\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms Chile at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 1,953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2023\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Release date - 19072023\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed Chile's Long-Term (LT) Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) and Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Issuer Default Ratings (IDRs) at 'A-' with a Stable Outlook.\par \par \uc2 A full list of rating actions is at the end of this rating action commentary.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Credit Strengths and Weaknesses: Chile's ratings are supported by a relatively strong sovereign balance sheet, with government debt/GDP well below peers, solid governance indicators and a track record of credible macroeconomic policies centered on an inflation-targeting regime and flexible exchange rate. These strengths are balanced by per capita income that \uc2 Fitch\uc2  projects will remain low relative to peers, high commodity dependence and weak external leverage and liquidity metrics.\par \par \uc2 Constitutional Reform Uncertain: Chile is preparing a second draft of a new constitution to be put to a vote this December following an unsuccessful first attempt in 2022. An expert commission finished a pre-draft in May that is more moderate and in line with the current charter than the first proposal. It includes principles that must be respected in the final text (e.g. property rights, Congress current structure, and BCCh independence) and some social rights subject to availability of resources. In \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's view, the new draft would not impair Chile's economic model or sustainability of public finances.\par \par \uc2 The final text will be prepared by the Constituent Council elected on May 7. The council is dominated by the political right, who won the two-thirds majority needed to control the council and make amendments to the pre-draft unopposed. Amid this backdrop, there could be risk that the final draft veers farther to the right, raising the chances of a rejection in the referendum. Early polls show that over half of Chileans would vote 'no' to the current draft.\par \par \uc2 Limited Reform Agenda Progress: President \uc2 Gabriel Boric\uc2  has had limited success on his tax reform agenda amid low popularity and political gridlock. While his government has pushed through some legislation in Congress (e.g. mining royalty bill), the rejection of his flagship tax reform (2.7% of GDP) in March was a major setback. The government will propose a new tax bill. Given the opposition's stance against new taxation, it is likely to focus on administrative measures and incentives for private investment. If passed, it is unclear how much revenue it could ultimately generate and whether it could fully fund President Boric's campaign promises for higher social spending. The government estimates that the mining royalty bill approved in May will yield 0.5% of GDP annually.\par \par \uc2 The government presented a pension reform bill in late 2022, which has not made much progress in Congress. The Boric administration's challenges in advancing his agenda and in raising taxes to address spending pressures could result in renewed social discontent and/or fiscal slippage.\par \par \uc2 Weaker Fiscal Balances: In line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), we forecast the general government (GG) balance to return to a deficit this year. However, we expect a larger deterioration, with the deficit at 2.7% of GDP compared with the MOF's 1.9%. While we forecast deficits to narrow over the forecast period through 2025, we expect them to remain above 2.0% of GDP. In 2023, tax receipts should continue adjusting to softer economic activity and falling copper revenues, though these will be partially offset by higher tax intake tied to lithium. Meanwhile expenses are rising due to higher pension benefits and a larger interest burden. The outlook faces downside risks as a stronger-than-expected El Nino could lead to additional spending.\par \par \uc2 Debt Prospect Deterioration: Over the medium term, we expect GG debt to GDP to continue rising given the level of budget deficits and sizable below the line items, i.e. capitalization of state-owned enterprises, student loan purchases, recognition bonds and replenishing sovereign funds. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  projects GG debt/GDP to rise to 39.7% of GDP at end-2023 from 38.0% in 2022 and to follow an upward path through the forecast period to 42.5% in 2025. This still compares favorably with the 'A' median of 50.6%, but the percentage of interest/revenues has surpassed the current 'A' median (3.6% versus 2.7%).\par \par \uc2 Softer Post-Pandemic Growth: We expect GDP growth to decelerate to 0% in 2023 before recovering to 2.4% in 2024, as activity rebalances due to declining private consumption and investment (likely also impacted by political uncertainty) amid a restrictive monetary policy stance. Activity should be supported by government consumption as counter-cyclical fiscal policy is implemented to contain a bigger economic correction. Net exports should also support GDP as Chile benefits from higher global demand for essential metals needed for the energy transition, offsetting a weaker than expected impact from reopening in China.\par \par \uc2 In the medium term, we see growth returning to the pre-pandemic trend of 2.0% (2015-2019), supported by copper production as new projects coming online offset near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenges and old project delays.\par \par \uc2 Monetary Policy to Ease: \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BCCh, has held the monetary policy rate at 11.25% since October 2022, which, coupled with lower fuel and food prices, a softer labor market, and a strengthening peso, has helped to lower inflation. With headline inflation falling to 7.6% in June from 12.8% in October and inflation expectations at the 12- and 24- month horizons closer to the 3% target midpoint, the BCCh indicated that the start of an easing cycle is near in its previous monetary policy meeting in June. We expect the BCCh to cut slowly as core inflation has fallen at a slower rate than headline and remained well above the target midpoint at 9.1% in June.\par \par \uc2 Moderating External Vulnerabilities: We expect the current account deficit to narrow substantially to 3.5% of GDP in 2023 from 9.0% in 2022 driven by an improvement in the trade balance as exports rise, buoyed by China's reopening despite fall in copper prices, and imports fall amid lower oil prices and household spending.\par \par \uc2 The BCCh plans to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by USD10 billion over June 2023 to June 2024 amid a weaker external liquidity position due to last year's USD25 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program. \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's access to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's Flexible Credit Line amounting to USD18.5 billion offers additional liquidity support.\par \par \uc2 ESG - Governance: Chile has an ESG Relevance Score (RS) of '5' for both Political Stability and Rights and '5' [+] for the Rule of Law, Institutional and Regulatory Quality and Control of Corruption. Theses scores reflect the high weight that the World Bank Governance Indicators (WBGI) have in our proprietary Sovereign Rating Model (SRM). Chile has a high WBGI ranking at 73.5, reflecting its long track record of stable and peaceful political transitions, well established rights for participation in the political process, strong institutional capacity, effective rule of law and a low level of corruption.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Negative Rating Action/Downgrade\par \par \uc2 Public Finances: Markedly higher government debt/GDP and/or depletion of sovereign assets, for example reflecting persistent large fiscal deficits driven by strong social spending pressures;\par \par \uc2 External Finances: Renewed sharp deterioration in external finances and liquidity, for example due to a copper price shock.\par \par \uc2 Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Positive Rating Action/Upgrade\par \par \uc2 Public Finances: A declining trajectory of government debt/GDP and/or a rebuilding of fiscal buffers, for example reflecting fiscal consolidation;\par \par \uc2 Macroeconomic: Improvement in medium-term economic growth prospects that helps raise GDP per capita, ease social tensions and improve fiscal dynamics.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign Rating Model (SRM) and Qualitative Overlay (QO)\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's proprietary SRM assigns Chile a score equivalent to a rating of 'BBB+' on the LT FC IDR scale.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's sovereign rating committee adjusted the output from the SRM to arrive at the final LT FC IDR by applying its qualitative overlay (QO) relative to the SRM output, as follows:\par \par \uc2 Macro: +1 notch added to offset the deterioration of inflation and GDP growth volatility variables in the SRM due to the current position in the economic cycle, which \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects will be temporary, and would otherwise add excess volatility to the rating.\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's SRM is the agency's proprietary multiple regression rating model that employs 18 variables based on three-year centered averages, including one year of forecasts, to produce a score equivalent to a LT FC IDR. \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's QO is a forward-looking qualitative framework designed to allow for adjustment to the SRM output to assign the final rating, reflecting factors within our criteria that are not fully quantifiable and/or not fully reflected in the SRM.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Sovereigns, Public Finance and Infrastructure issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of three notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\par \par \uc2 The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Chile has an ESG Relevance Score of '5' for Political Stability and Rights as WBGIs have the highest weight in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's SRM and are therefore highly relevant to the rating and a key rating driver with a high weight. As Chile has a percentile rank below 50 for the respective Governance Indicator, this has a negative impact on the credit profile.\par \par \uc2 Chile has an ESG Relevance Score of '5' [+] for Rule of Law, Institutional & Regulatory Quality and Control of Corruption as WBGIs have the highest weight in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's SRM and are therefore highly relevant to the rating and are a key rating driver with a high weight. As Chile has a percentile rank above 50 for the respective Governance Indicators, this has a positive impact on the credit profile.\par \par \uc2 Chile has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' [+] for Human Rights and Political Freedoms as the Voice and Accountability pillar of the WBGIs is relevant to the rating and a rating driver. As Chile has a percentile rank above 50 for the respective Governance Indicator, this has a positive impact on the credit profile.\par \par \uc2 Chile has an ESG Relevance Score of '4' [+] for Creditor Rights as willingness to service and repay debt is relevant to the rating and is a rating driver for Chile, as for all sovereigns. As Chile has track record of 20+ years without a restructuring of public debt and captured in our SRM variable, this has a positive impact on the credit profile.\par \par \uc2 Except for the matters discussed above, the highest level of ESG credit relevance, if present, is a score of '3'. This means ESG issues are credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or to the way in which they are being managed by the entity. For more information on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's ESG Relevance Scores, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com/esg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com/esg}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020230720ej7k0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
