\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 B. Central: Current account recorded a deficit equivalent to 9% of GDP in 2022\b0\par\par\uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 During the year 2022, the current account registered a deficit of US$27,102 million, equivalent to 9% of the gross domestic product (GDP), informed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this Monday through the document Balance of Payments, International Investment Position and External Debt corresponding to the mentioned fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 The result was explained by the income from foreign investment in Chile and the deficit of the services trade balance; meanwhile, the goods trade balance was positive.\par \par \uc2 The financial account recorded net capital inflows of US$25,387 million, determined by the decrease in reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BCCh), as part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, and by direct and portfolio foreign investment in \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the end of the year, the net international investment position (\uc2 IIP\uc2 ) increased its debit balance with respect to 2021, to US$55,435 million (18.1% of GDP, in annual terms). The result was due to the indebtedness of the financial account and the decline in the valuation of assets.\par \par \uc2 The external debt, as compared to the end of 2021, decreased to US$233,325 million (76.4% of GDP), due to the fall in the value of government and corporate bonds. Meanwhile, the residual short-term external debt stood at US$68,234 million, mainly due to payments by non-financial companies.\par \par \uc2 Goods trade balance\par \par \uc2 The balance of trade in goods recorded a surplus of US$3,807 million in the period, following the increase in exports and imports.\par \par \uc2 Shipments of goods amounted to US$98,548 million, representing an increase of 4.0% over the previous year, explained by a 5.5% increase in prices and a 1.5% drop in volume terms. The result was driven by shipments of industrial products, while mining and forestry and livestock exports declined.\par \par \uc2 Mining exports fell by 2.9% to US$56,489 million. The result was explained by lower copper prices and, to a lesser extent, lower volumes. This effect was partly offset by lithium carbonate exports, which rose sharply, in line with international prices.\par \par \uc2 Shipments of agricultural, forestry and extractive fishing products fell by 1.6%, totaling US$6,643 million. The result reflected lower exports of fruit, mainly blueberries and apples; this effect was partially offset by higher shipments of cherries.\par \par \uc2 Industrial shipments grew by 18.6% to US$35,417 million. The boost came mainly from the chemical industry, where lithium-derived products stood out, and from the food industry, led by salmon shipments.\par \par \uc2 Imports of goods totaled US$94,741 (FOB) and US$104,530 million (CIF).\par \par \uc2 Compared to the same period of the previous year, and considering the results in CIF terms, imports registered an increase of 13.1%, reflecting a 14.9% increase in prices, while in terms of volume, they fell by 1.5%. The increase in intermediate goods predominated in the result; to a lesser extent, imports of consumer and capital goods also contributed.\par \par \uc2 Imports of intermediate goods grew 22.1%, totaling US$56,268 million. The result was led by imports of energy goods, where diesel was the most important. The rest of the intermediate goods also grew, driven mainly by chemical products.\par \par \uc2 Imports of consumer goods increased by 3.2% to US$28,505 million. The impulse came from imports of non-durable consumer goods, especially clothing and gasoline. Meanwhile, there was a generalized fall in imports of durable goods, with the exception of automobiles, which increased.\par \par \uc2 Imports of capital goods registered a level of US$19,757 million, which represented an increase of 5.6%, led by increased purchases of buses, trucks and cargo vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Services trade balance\par \par \uc2 The services balance recorded a deficit of US$14,824 million. Exports totaled US$8,529 million and imports US$23,353 million, after growing 42.8% and 27.7%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Both exports and imports were driven by an increase in all categories: Transportation, Travel and Other services.\par \par \uc2 In the case of exports, the result was led by air transport services, mainly for passengers. There was also an increase in air operating leasing services and business, professional and technical services.\par \par \uc2 Imports were led by maritime freight services, in line with the high level of international prices in this market. Imports of financial services and tourism services also increased.\par \par \uc2 Income\par \par \uc2 Net income presented a negative balance of US$16,520 million, determined by the income accrued from foreign direct investment in Chile.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, income from Chilean investments abroad presented an increase, in line with the performance of investments associated with the maritime transport, energy and mining sectors.\par \par \uc2 Transfers\par \par \uc2 Net transfers recorded a surplus of US$434 million. The recovery of the balance with respect to the previous year is explained by the normalization of personal remittances abroad, after they increased in line with the partial withdrawals of pension funds and fiscal aid.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230320ej3k00649\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
