\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  warns that "the last traces of Chile's reputation as the most stable country in Latin America are being erased".\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Chile - The instability experienced during the last few months in Chilean assets "are erasing the last traces of the country's reputation as the most stable in Latin America," warned \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In its analysis, the agency indicated that the volatility of the Chilean peso is the highest in the world after the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while local swap rates have oscillated at an almost unprecedented pace. That unpredictability has pushed many investors to the sidelines, with the nation's sovereign dollar debt now trading at levels similar to the Philippines, rated two notches below Chile.\par \par \uc2 Chile's fall from grace has been a long time coming. It began even before the social outbreak three years ago that paralyzed much of the country and shocked investors dazzled by a sense of security from three decades of steady growth and low inflation. Now, there seems to be no remedy for the decline. Even a new constitution that many had warned would undermine investment and growth only announced further declines last month.\par \par \uc2 "Chile used to be synonymous with stability and security, but now that's a distant memory," said Mario Castro, a fixed-income strategist at \uc2 Banco Bilbao Vizcaya Argentaria SA\uc2  in New York. "There has been a structural change in Chile, involving institutional deterioration and a shift toward a welfare state that will increase fiscal pressure."\par \par \uc2 Volatility has hit new highs recently as emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hit by a stronger dollar at a time when Chile struggles to finance one of the world's highest current-account deficits. That pushed the peso to record lows in July and triggered the largest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's history.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rated AA- by S&P Global, on par with market favorites such as Taiwan and the Czech Republic. Then, a growing fiscal deficit led to a downgrade to A+ that year, followed by another cut to A in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Now, even that rating seems to flatter Chile, with its dollar debt trading at yields similar to BBB-rated countries like the Philippines, and well above Saudi Arabia, according to data compiled by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There are downward pressures on Chile's rating," said \uc2 Valerie Ho\uc2 , portfolio manager at Doubleline Group LP in Los Angeles. "Chile's vulnerabilities are a weaker growth outlook, uncertainty about future policy in the mining sector and spending plans."\par \par \uc2 Chile's Finance Ministry\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 What really shook Chile and the markets out of their 30-year slumber were the October 2019 riots that brought the military back onto the streets and saw the then-president nearly besieged in La Moneda palace. "Nothing has been the same since," \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  highlights.\par \par \uc2 The market-friendly policies that have characterized the country since the 1980s were questioned, as were the parties that have dominated politics since the end of \uc2 Augusto Pinochet\uc2 's dictatorship in 1990.\par \par \uc2 Now, President \uc2 Gabriel Boric\uc2  heads Chile's most leftist government in half a century, pushing for constitutional change, while seeking to increase taxes on the mining industry, improve labor rights and redesign a pension system based on personal savings. All this against the backdrop of stagnant growth, the fastest inflation in three decades and a soaring current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 Chile's current account gap will narrow sharply next year as the economy contracts while inflation slows. At the same time, many expect the price of copper, which accounts for about half of exports, to improve, while production of the metal should increase.\par \par \uc2 In addition, Chile is already one of the few countries in the world expected to present a nearly balanced budget this year.\par \par \uc2 "Chile will improve over time and will look closer to what it was before," said Andres Abadia, economist at Pantheon Economics. "However, returning to being a safe haven in Latin America will depend on how the rules of the game are maintained in the new constitution."\par \par \uc2 For now, investors are struggling to understand all the changes. The peso's 30-day volatility has outpaced that of the Brazilian real and Colombian peso.\par \par \uc2 "For many years, inflation was basically nowhere and rates were not moving," said Carlos Fernandez-Aller, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and emerging markets macro \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 . "A five basis point move in Chile was a big deal. Now it's a completely, completely different story."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221013eiac003qn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
