\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 War and pandemic return Mexico and Latin America to era of high rates\b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 In the fight against pandemic inflation, Latin America led the world into a new era of money scarcity. Eighteen months later, there is no sign that being the first in will help the region become the first out.\par \par \uc2 Last August 11, the Bank of Mexico and Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced what were their tenth and thirteenth consecutive interest rate hikes, respectively. Forecasters do not believe that any of them are over yet. In Brazil, one of the world's first hikers in March 2021, there is a chance it could stop. Still, specialists said this week they expect to keep rates in "significantly contractionary territory for a sufficiently prolonged period."\par \par \uc2 Latin America's central bankers, of course, are not the only ones who missed their targets by a mile during the worst price crisis in decades. But, amid signs that the inflationary wave may be peaking, they have a particular reason to remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 A local history of hyperinflation means that even simple contracts such as rents are tied to past price increases, helping inflation to take hold. in the opposite direction to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies that attempt to control demand.\par \par \uc2 'It has to wait'And on top of that, the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mostly strong performers this year thanks to higher rates and commodity prices, have been wobbling again in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  says..."Inflation resilience reflects the lag between rate hikes and their effect on the economy. Those effects are held back by two other factors: the adverse external scenario, which weakens emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushes up tradable prices; in addition, fiscal measures adopted by some Latin economies fuel the very inflation whose effects they seek to alleviate."\par \par \uc2 --Adriana Dupita, Latin American economist.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls, especially when the US and other developed economies are raising their own interest rates, are a classic path to runaway prices in Latin America. That gives central bankers another incentive to keep policy tight, and perhaps use other tools as well. \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last month and others may follow suit.\par \par \uc2 "There is a possibility that many may have to wait until 2024 to cut rates," says Gabriel Casillas, chief economist for Latin America at Barclays Plc. Economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  suggest easing could begin a bit sooner, but not before the second half of next year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation continues to accelerate in many Latin American nations, with Mexico and Chile recently posting multi-decade highs. In Argentina, which has endured two decades of wild economic swings, it topped 70 percent, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates by nearly 10 percentage points on Thursday. Overall, the region has the world's fastest price growth outside war-torn Eastern Europe, data compiled by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  show.\par \par \uc2 Brazil experienced a sharp drop in prices last month, though it was largely due to energy tax cuts pushed by President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2 , who is running for re-election in October.\par \par \uc2 'Part of the toolkit'Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also at risk from the end of the pandemic commodity boom, as well as from US interest rate hikes that will continue well into 2023.\par \par \uc2 For most of the year, they have held up better than most against the rising dollar. Brazil's real and Mexico's peso are among a handful of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that posted gains, although they have lost ground from mid-year highs.\par \par \uc2 Others have seen steeper declines. \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversed the peso's slide in mid-July by announcing it would spend $25 billion over several weeks to buy it.\par \par \uc2 With interest rates already high enough to threaten economic growth, and many Latin American countries holding large amounts of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other central banks may resort to such measures as part of the fight against inflation, says Daniel Titelman of the \uc2 Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We've seen large enough depreciations, in a region that, generally speaking, has accumulated reserves," he says. "That's part of the toolkit you have, in the same way that Chile did."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220813ei8d00612\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
