\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Rosanna Costa, president BCCh: 'I am the person of the crises'.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,268 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 (Bloomberg) -- Just hours after speaking to Bloomberg Markets, Rosanna Costa extinguished a looming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis by announcing the biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The only hint of the drama unfolding in the background were occasional interruptions from her assistant asking her to verify the details of a document. Smiling, polite, the president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Chile\uc2  apologized as she moved into an adjoining room. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked calm in the midst of an increasingly frantic market.\par \par \uc2 Costa, 64, has experience in crisis management. Days before he took over as director of budgets at the Finance Ministry in 2010, Chile was rocked by one of the biggest earthquakes in history. The cost of the damage amounted to billions of dollars. Since becoming a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker in January 2017, she has had to deal with the impact of a wave of social protests over inequality and poor public services that paralyzed the country and often turned violent. More recently, he helped the economy navigate through the covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Since taking over as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president in February, Costa has raised interest rates to the highest level in more than two decades in an attempt to curb the fastest inflation in a generation, as well as the threat to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-July. Moreover, as a former member of a right-wing government, he is directing monetary policy after Chileans voted in the most left-wing government in 50 years, electing \uc2 Gabriel Boric\uc2  as the country's president.\par \par \uc2 "I seem to be the crisis person," Costa told Bloomberg Markets. The interview, which took place on July 14, has been edited for length and content.\par \par \uc2 BLOOMBERG MARKETS: How do you feel at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after having a very different job as deputy director of the \uc2 Institute for Liberty\uc2  and Development, a free-market think tank?\par \par \uc2 ROSANNA COSTA: I knew the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was my first job after leaving university. I was here for about 10 years and I would say that the bank was my second school, especially when you come straight out of university, the work makes its mark.\par \par \uc2 BM: Did you ever plan to be president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RC: It was never in my thoughts. I think that in each of the jobs I have had, I have looked for the space to grow and contribute. And, above all, to enjoy the job, beyond the tensions or the complexity of it. When I refer to enjoyment, I'm talking about being passionate about what you are doing.\par \par \uc2 BM: Are you passionate about what you are doing today as president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RC: The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offers a platform to be able to contribute to your country. There are many areas of work within the bank. Today, inflation has been presented as a phenomenon, but it was absent for many years. You can see the cost to families, to households, and I have a great platform to contribute to the fight against that. That motivates me.\par \par \uc2 BM: Do you still enjoy it when you have days when it seems like a crisis is looming like today?\par \par \uc2 RC: Stress management is something you learn over time, and I'm old enough to know that. That's not to say it doesn't exist, but you know and recognize yourself, and you find your own way to manage it. I have a very supportive family; I have important emotional support. You learn to manage stress and balance life.\par \par \uc2 BM: When you get home from work, do you still read academic papers or do you find a way to relax and do other things?\par \par \uc2 RC: This is getting into the more intimate territory of my home. I don't easily disconnect from work, not at all. But I really enjoy family time, family gatherings. Having my own family close by is the most valuable thing to me.\par \par \uc2 BM: It is currently such a tumultuous time for the markets that there is a lot of contradictory information. How do you handle all the data?\par \par \uc2 RC: We work within a structure. We apply or make decisions today so that their effects are seen over time. We work based on what is the medium-term perspective, so we have to understand very well what is happening, what are the forces behind it and how to project it forward. It is a challenge.\par \par \uc2 Working in the unknown is always a challenge. Controlling inflation two years in advance helps us avoid overreacting in the present. We have very well-prepared technical teams. There are top-level management teams overseeing that work. There is a bureau that, as a whole, is a group made up of people who know a lot about the issues and are very committed. Together we make decisions that are as informed as possible.\par \par \uc2 BM: Do you read all the bank analysts' reports that so often tell you what to do, and do you consider all the opinions of the market and investors?\par \par \uc2 RC: We don't have our windows closed, but we don't read everything that is published either. We look at what is said, see if the arguments make sense. We contrast with what we have and with what the evidence tells us, knowing that the evidence tells us about the past and that we are building the future. And, if there is relevant information, we take it into account, obviously without getting dizzy.\par \par \uc2 BM: What qualities do you consider you have that help you deal with crises or difficult times?\par \par \uc2 RC: I am a person who listens a lot and who seeks to generate agreement among the board members. But I am also a person who makes decisions when it is time to make them. This is an institution that makes its decisions collegially, so it is important to build trust and work together. That is something that excites me, something I am born to do.\par \par \uc2 BM: How does it feel to be the first woman president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to work in an environment historically dominated by men?\par \par \uc2 RC: I was once told that I could easily work in teams of men and that the difference was not very noticeable. I don't know what they meant by that [laughs]. In my family we were only two sisters, but I was always very close to my father. At that time he studied to be a commercial engineer at the university, since pure economics did not exist in Chile. I also went to a coeducational school.\par \par \uc2 BM: During the social unrest of October 2019, you were a member of the bank's board of directors. Were the riots a shock for you?\par \par \uc2 RC: The way the riots manifested themselves was a shock.\par \par \uc2 BM: Did it change in any way the opinion you have about Chile's economic model?\par \par \uc2 RC: I can't really answer that as president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BM: I guess that is one of the main differences between your work here and your time in the think tank. You cannot freely express your political views.\par \par \uc2 RC: They are different institutions. But the exposure, the public part of that work, was never what attracted me most. I was interested in the research work.\par \par \uc2 Original Note:\par \par \uc2 Chile \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Rosanna Costa: 'I'm the Person of Crises'.\par \par \uc2 More stories like this are available on bloomberg.com\par \par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A92022 \uc2 Bloomberg L.P\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220803ei8300el3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
