\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The Big Mac Index\b0\par\par\uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Big Mac Index was invented by The Economist in 1986, according to its website, as a light-hearted guide to determine whether countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quotes worldwide are at their "correct" level or are "undervalued" or "overvalued." It is based on the theory of purchasing power parity (PPP), which says that price levels between two countries should tend to be equal in the long run, i.e., an identical basket of goods in each country will cost the same once the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been exchanged.\par \par \uc2 The Economist does not use a basket but a world standard hamburger, the Big Mac which, for example, as of June 2022 cost 3,400 pesos in Chile, while in the US its price was US$5.15. The relation between both prices gives us a Big Mac exchange rate of 660.19 pesos, which would be its "correct" level. Now, if we compare this level with the official quotation of the Chilean peso as of June, which was 928.43 pesos for one dollar, we find that the official Chilean peso would be weaker, more Chilean pesos for one dollar with respect to its "correct value", by 28.9%.\par \par \uc2 This situation occurs, according to The Economist website, with the majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  worldwide that would be in a race to devalue, with the exception of a few \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Swiss franc, which would be rather "above its value" by 30.3%, the Norwegian krone (21.6%) and the Uruguayan peso (18.1%).\par \par \uc2 The underlying problem is the revaluation of the U.S. dollar, the highest in 20 years, and the fact that the rival \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro, from a ratio of US$ 1.15 in December 2021 fell in a few months to a rate of US$ 0.98, less than one dollar for one euro. There are many explanations for the revaluation of the dollar; one of them, that it is "a post-pandemic evil" due to the excess of spending, and another, which associates it to the problem of inflation and the increase in interest rates by the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, it is convenient to analyze the behavior of the dollar not in nominal terms with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but in real terms; which means taking into account the relation of prices with other economies, for which the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed) calculates a Real Broad Dollar Index, where levels above 100 mean a "real revaluation". If we start in January 2006 with an index equal to 100, we find that the index as of June 2022 is 115, which reflects a real appreciation of 15%.\par \par \uc2 First of all it should be noted that a strong dollar is neither recent nor is it post-pandemic, since its trend is already observed as of March 2015, which continued with COVID-19 in 2020 and continues until now. Therefore, it seems unreasonable to associate the real appreciation of the dollar only to increased fiscal spending in times of pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Second, while there is a relationship between dollar appreciation and the Fed's interest rate, it is worth keeping in mind that prior to its rate hike in mid-March, already the real appreciation as of February 2022 was 10%. Then, the increase of its interest rate from 0.25% in March to 1.15% in June shows an additional appreciation of the dollar of 5%, so it would not be the only explanation for its appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The context is undoubtedly high inflation and the problem is that central banks believe that in order to curb inflation, interest rates must be raised, on the understanding that it is a demand-only inflation due to the expansion of the quantity of money, so by raising their interest rates they make money expensive and lower prices.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, several central banks in South America let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate due to the recent fall in commodity prices and although the devaluation nominally encourages exports, on the other hand, it makes imports more expensive, so they had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their exchange markets, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 Bolivia is not yet in the middle of the vortex of high inflation and strong depreciation, so we must preserve the economic stability achieved as a heritage of all Bolivians.\par \par \uc2 Gabriel Loza Teller\u237\'00\'EDa is an economist, self-employed and Bolivarian.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220727ei7r0078u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
