\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Rtgs: Chile, Colombia Large Current Account Deficits Are a Vulnerability\b0\par\par\uc2 2,026 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2022\par 16:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 26 Jul 2022 10:28 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2   Rtgs: Chile, Colombia Large Current Account Deficits Are a Vulnerability\par \par \uc2 The following is a press release from \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  -London-26 July 2022: Chile's (A-/Stable) and Colombia's (BB+/Stable) current account deficits (CADs) will remain large in 2022 due to continued strong import growth and high profit remittances by companies in the commodities sector, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. Large CADs are a vulnerability as external financing conditions tighten, and if domestic political uncertainty begins to weigh on FDI prospects, external pressures could build. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has revised its forecasts for both countries' 2022 CADs. We now see Chile's at 6.2% of GDP, compared with 6.4% in 2021. For Colombia, our updated 2022 forecast sees the CAD little changed, at 5.6% of GDP from 5.7%. We had forecast the CADs to moderate this year to 4.1% and 4.6%, respectively, on higher prices for commodity exports and lower imports. Our new forecasts reflect continued high domestic demand driving strong import growth while commodity export prices are volatile. Copper prices have fallen to pre-Ukraine war levels and oil prices have retreated from their peak. Profit remittances from private oil and mining companies remain another significant factor.\par \par \uc2 Wide CADs increase vulnerability to tighter global financing conditions and a global economic slowdown and possible recession. Political uncertainties are a further consideration, with a new leftist government about to take office in Colombia, while in Chile there is a coming referendum on a new constitution. Combined with the tougher global backdrop these uncertainties could affect the financial accounts through both FDI and portfolio flows. Chile's proposed new constitution will be put to a popular referendum in early September and irrespective of its outcome, uncertainties will persist. In Chile, the administration of \uc2 Gabriel Boric\uc2  that took office in March has thus far demonstrated fiscal pragmatism, committing to tax increases to meet social spending pressures. But its ability to deliver these remains unclear in the face of a split Congress. Similarly in Colombia, the appointment of finance minister Jose Antonio Ocampo suggests a pragmatic approach. But while incoming president Gustavo Petro has toned down his initial rhetoric, his proposal to halt new oil exploration has weighed on investor sentiment, and details on tax and pension reforms are yet to be worked out. The Chilean peso and Colombian peso have come under greater pressure in the past month than many other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of a stronger U.S. dollar. Depreciation should help to facilitate external adjustment over time, but can amplify existing macroeconomic challenges by pushing inflation higher.\par \par \uc2 It also directly affects debt/GDP ratios. Colombia and Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  composition deteriorated during the Covid-19 pandemic as they relied increasingly on external debt to fund their large fiscal deficits. Chile's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  share of debt rose to nearly 35% at end-2021 from 21% at end-2019, while Colombia's rose to about 40% from close to 34%. Central banks have been proactive, raising rates aggressively and helping to support policy credibility. But reserves have still fallen, notably in Chile, from a peak of USD55 billion in October 2021 to USD45 billion in June 2022. \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this month, announcing USD10 billion of sales in the spot market and USD10 billion in hedging instruments until end-September and a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap plan for up to USD5 billion to boost liquidity in dollars complemented by a liquidity program in pesos. This has boosted the peso, but will weaken external liquidity metrics. And Chile replaced its USD23.9 billion \uc2 IMF\uc2  flexible credit line (FCL), with a much smaller USD3.5 billion short-term facility. Colombia has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to date. Its external liquidity metrics are stronger with reserve coverage of current account payments at more than seven months (versus just over three for Chile). Colombia has maintained a USD9.8 billion IMF FCL, giving it an additional external buffer. Contacts: Richard Francis Senior Director, Sovereigns +1 212 908 0858 Fitch Ratings, Inc. Hearst Tower 300 West 57th Street New York, NY 10014 Shelly Shetty Managing Director, Sovereigns +1 212 908 0324 Mark Brown Senior Director, Fitch Wire +44 20 3530 1588\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Elizabeth Fogerty, New York, Tel: +1 212 908 0526, Email: elizabeth.fogerty@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 All \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  (\uc2 Fitch\uc2  ) credit ratings are subject to certain limitations and disclaimers. Please read these limitations and disclaimers by following this link: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/understandingcreditratings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http