\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention \highlight\uc2  in the U.S. dollar market does not impress swap rates\b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BCCh) began its largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in history last week. The dollar market reacted strongly, but the effect on swap rates was more diluted.\par \par \uc2 Two-year peso-Camero swap rates fell only 11 basis points to 9.71%, down from the previous week's high of 10.05%, but up from 8.39% at the beginning of the month. More notably, five-year rates rose 18 basis points from the previous week to 7.49%, even as \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  rates fell.\par \par \uc2 The BCCh unveiled a US$25 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program on July 14, triggering a sharp rebound in the peso from historic lows. But the dollar sales and an increase in the monetary policy rate failed to ensure that inflation will decelerate soon, or that an end to tighter monetary policy is in sight.\par \par \uc2 "The shorter part of the swap curve fell because a stronger peso should reduce pressure on inflation, but that good run was not enough to benefit longer swaps," said Sebastian Ide, manager of \uc2 Banco de Chile\uc2 's money desk. The long end of the curve was more impacted by local risk factors, he said.\par \par \uc2 Investors remain concerned that the peso will fall again against the dollar when \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends in September, considering that \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  is running its largest current account deficit since at least 1997. There are also concerns about the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is equivalent to almost half of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves.\par \par \uc2 "The risks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's future credibility could be very high," said Felipe Alarc\u243\'00\'F3n, chief economist at Euroamerica in Santiago. "It is critical that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is successful."\par \par \uc2 Daily salesOn top of this, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week will be lower: US$150 million a day in the spot market and US$350 million in forwards, compared to US$200 million and US$500 million, respectively, last week.\par \par \uc2 Two-year inflation breakevens, a gauge of price expectations over that period, fell to 7.1% last week from 7.6% the previous week.\par \par \uc2 "Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a lower dollar, there are still strong upside risks to consumer prices," said Jaime Achondo, general manager of brokerage Fynsa. Among these are possible increases in transport and energy prices, and further economic stimulus from the government.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Gabriel Boric\uc2  this month announced an additional US$1.2 billion aid package, which the government insists will not impact inflation. This comes on top of a US$3.7 billion package announced in April. In addition, the government may unfreeze public transport prices from 2023, said Finance Minister \uc2 Mario Marcel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, some think the breakevens should compress in the coming weeks as inflation nears a turning point.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation could be close to peaking, with the economy likely to enter recession by the end of the year," said Mariano Alvarez, fixed income manager at LarrainVial. "Betting on nominal bonds over CPI-indexed bonds is starting to make sense."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220725ei7p005im\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
