\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Strong dollar rattles much of Latin America's economy\b0\par\par\uc2 1,646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Luis Alejandro Amaya E.\par \par \uc2 Redacci\u243\'00\'F3n Am\u233\'00\'E9rica, Jul 23 (EFE) - The U.S. dollar has experienced an unusual appreciation in recent weeks that has caused several Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to devalue, with the inevitable consequences that this entails for the public coffers and domestic economies of the countries in the region.\par \par \uc2 A large part of the products that make up the family basket in Latin America are imported and paid for in dollars, as well as important resources such as hydrocarbons and raw materials.\par \par \uc2 A POST-PANDEMIC EVIL\par \par \uc2 "After a pandemic in which the economy was stimulated comes a great phenomenon called inflation. When central banks start to raise interest rates and begin to see a possible recession, growth has to be curbed," financial analyst Andres Moreno told EFE.\par \par \uc2 "There was runaway growth in the world, everything recovered faster than expected. With better interest rates and a possible recession, international capital is more risk-averse and prefers to go to more solid economies. This translates into a devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of emerging countries," he adds.\par \par \uc2 Another important factor in the depreciation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar is the constant interest rate hikes by the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  (Fed), the last one on June 16 to 1.50%.\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR: ANOTHER PAIN FOR ARGENTINA\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, the strengthening of the dollar adds to the country's macroeconomic imbalances and reinforces the weakness of the peso itself, as a result of 64% year-on-year inflation, the shortage of international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , devaluation expectations, the fiscal deficit and political uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar is going to build on a problem that Argentina had before," said Gabriel Caama\u241\'00\'F1o, an economist at the Ledesma consulting firm, to EFE, who warns that there are already discussions that inflation could reach three digits.\par \par \uc2 Argentina applies strong exchange controls to authorize the entry and exit of dollars at the official exchange rate, which has led to a proliferation of parallel exchange rates on the stock market and the black market.\par \par \uc2 The expectations of devaluation and the weakness of the Argentine peso are reflected in a gap of more than 147 % between the official exchange rate and the "blue" or parallel exchange rate, which this week reached 338 pesos.\par \par \uc2 STRONG \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IN \b \highlight22\uc2 CHILE\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Alarm bells went off in \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  on July 14, when the dollar reached an all-time high of 1,051 pesos. This dizzying escalation precipitated that same day a historic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for an amount of 25 billion dollars until September.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is proving to be "successful", as it has managed in just four days to push the dollar back below 1,000 pesos, according to experts.\par \par \uc2 "Before the exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso was among the most devalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world, despite the fact that Chile has relatively stable and healthy economic fundamentals. The appreciation of the dollar had reached 20%, but thanks to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  it has been reduced to 12%," Francisco Casta\u241\'00\'F1eda, of the Universidad Mayor, explained to EFE.\par \par \uc2 COLOMBIA: A DOLLAR WITH POLITICAL INFLUENCE?\par \par \uc2 The dollar in Colombia reached a record high of 4,625 pesos on July 12, driven by world volatility due to fears that the United States could enter a recession that could turn global.\par \par \uc2 The country will be governed as from August 7 by the leftist \uc2 Gustavo Petro\uc2 , but market analysts consider that the political situation has little influence on the behavior of the exchange rate, since the Colombian peso has accumulated so far this year a depreciation of 10.5% in the official quotation.\par \par \uc2 "Part of what is happening in Colombia and Chile is that they are countries highly exposed to raw materials: oil in the case of Colombia and copper in the case of Chile, and both have had price reversals. This means that we have two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with high sensitivity in this environment", says the chief economist of \uc2 BBVA Research\uc2 , Alejandro Reyes.\par \par \uc2 So far, Banco de la Rep\u250\'00\'FAblica's policy of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the value of the dollar has been successful and, after last week's record high, the dollar has entered an adjustment trend, with four days of declines, and last Thursday it closed at 4,410 pesos.\par \par \uc2 THE RESILIENT MEXICAN PESO\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso closed at 20.51 per dollar on July 19, the same figure as on December 31, 2021, according to official data from the \uc2 Bank of Mexico (Banxico)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Janneth Quiroz, chief economist at \uc2 Monex Group\uc2 , emphasizes to EFE that in the first half of the year the Mexican peso maintained the levels of the end of 2021 with an appreciation of 0.1%, closing at 20.13 pesos per U.S. dollar on June 30.\par \par \uc2 PERUVIAN SOL SOMEWHAT OPAQUE AGAINST THE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 In Peru, in the first days of July, the exchange rate of the dollar experienced an increase of 3.8 % on average, in the interbank quotation, towards the end of the period from July 1 to July 12.\par \par \uc2 In those days, 3.97 soles had to be given per dollar, as opposed to 3.82 soles on average last June, according to figures from the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  (BCRP).\par \par \uc2 THE DOLLAR DECLINES IN BRAZIL\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suffered a merry-go-round and, contrary to the rest of the region, accumulated a decline of only 2.43% from January to July 20.\par \par \uc2 However, since last May, the dollar has gradually strengthened in the Brazilian market, after having lost up to 15.35 %, although the impact of the rise of the greenback has not been as great as in some of the neighboring countries.\par \par \uc2 According to Joelson Sampaio, professor at the \uc2 Getulio Vargas Foundation (FGV)\uc2 , the strengthening of the dollar should continue in Brazil and other emerging countries in the face of "the expectation" of a further increase in interest rates and inflation in the US.\par \par \uc2 THE "DOLLARIZED" COUNTRIES\par \par \uc2 In Ecuador, the appreciation of the dollar has generated concern due to the rising cost of exports, and, although the measure benefits, on the other hand, imports, domestic sales depend on the purchasing power in a country in recurrent economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 "We are very concerned because we are living through times that have not been seen in the last two decades", with an appreciation of the dollar "as strong as the one that is happening", the president of the Ecuadorian Federation of Exports (Fedexpor), Felipe Ribadeneira, told EFE.\par \par \uc2 The strengthening of the dollar means that Ecuadorian exports are losing competitiveness in destination countries, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are depreciating.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the revaluation of the dollar hardly affects Panama, and rather benefits it, since it is a country of services and more of an importer than an exporter.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Ecuador and El Salvador, Panama has had the dollar since its foundation as an independent state after its separation from Colombia in 1903.\par \par \uc2 The high value of the dollar has not affected El Salvador's exports, which in fact have remained on the rise despite inflation.\par \par \uc2 According to the Central Reserve Bank, total exports between January and June 2022 totaled US$3,256 million, with a 16.8% growth.\par \par \uc2 In addition, imports reached $8,934.4 million, registering a 27.2 % increase. And although bitcoin is a legal tender since 2021, the economy is not "bitcoinized".\par \par \uc2 Ricardo Castaneda, coordinator of the Central American Institute for Fiscal Studies (Icefi) for El Salvador and Honduras, tells EFE that the incidence or effect on the economy in general is minimal, due to the dollarization in force since 2001.\par \par \uc2 However, this could affect groups such as exporters who do not send their products to the U.S. but to other countries, as their products will be less competitive.\par \par \uc2 THE EURO IS THE PROBLEM IN CUBA\par \par \uc2 In Cuba the dollar has not had a significant rise, but the euro has registered a steady rise for the past month, above the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Until last Tuesday, the European bill was exchanged in the informal market at 124 Cuban pesos (cup). The official rate is 24.54 pesos.\par \par \uc2 The increase of the euro began in mid-June, coinciding with the entry into force of a directive of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Cuba\uc2  (BCC) that prevents consulates from changing to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the income from procedures invoiced in cup.\par \par \uc2 A few days later, several consulates confirmed that they would charge in euros. Many Cubans travel to Central American countries to make their way to the United States.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the US dollar has also risen in the informal market during 2022, but at a slower pace than the euro.\par \par \uc2 At the beginning of 2021, the country launched a package of economic reforms known as Tarea de Ordenamiento, which established an official exchange rate of 24 cupS for each dollar. However, on the street the greenback was trading on Tuesday at 118.\par \par \uc2 A DOLLAR THAT HURTS THE U.S. ITSELF.\par \par \uc2 The United States closed 2021 with a trade deficit of 27 % and this trend could continue this year if the dollar remains strong against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Broadly speaking, with a weaker euro, European goods and services become more attractive to the United States, while domestic producers become much less competitive internationally.\par \par \uc2 This could affect domestic production in sectors such as machinery, vehicles or the chemical industry, major sectors within U.S. exports to Europe.\par \par \uc2 "Europe today has a little bit more inflation, but it's in a stronger position in terms of international competition. So it's going to be a little easier to export and a little less attractive for them to import products from the U.S.," Carol Osler, professor of Financial Markets and Institutions at Brandeis International Business University, told EFE. EFE\par \par \uc2 The post Strong dollar shakes much of Latin America's economy first appeared on Ovaciones.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220723ei7n007hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
