\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Will Chile's rising dollar and inflation be brought under control?\b0\par\par\uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The main economic concern in Chile today is the strong inflation we are experiencing, 12.5% annualized, as of June of this year. The pressure of internal prices is so high that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the Monetary Policy Rate, TPM, on May 9 to 9% and has just raised it (July 13, 2022) by 75 points, to 9.75%, which reduces once again the money supply and will raise again the interest rates of the Chilean economy which, in June 2022, were at 26.3% the consumer rate and 12.99% the commercial rate. Actions aimed at reducing domestic demand (consumption and investment) and productive activity to reduce inflation.\par \par \uc2 However, the results have been slow, we are observing a slow adjustment of the IMACEC in 2022, from 6.8% in February to 6.40% in May, while the unemployment rate is also adjusting slowly, while prices have not stopped growing, moreover, recent data indicate that, in June, inflation, annualized, reached 12.5%. This indicates that inflationary pressures in Chile were not only due to internal factors (pension withdrawals -savings- and more fiscal spending, in universal FPI, for example), which translated at the time (2021) into a strong increase in the demand for consumer and capital goods and services, impacting prices. If this were the case, the results in inflationary control would have already been noticed.\par \par \uc2 We know that our prices are also rising due to external factors such as the increase in the price of our imports: oil, wheat and others; due to the increase in costs, due to problems in transportation logistics, which still persist; due to the reappearance of outbreaks of the pandemic, due to Russia's invasion war in Ukraine, which still continues, among others.\par \par \uc2 Higher values mean higher input values and higher domestic production costs (such as the higher price of wheat, which pushes up the price of bread) or raise the prices of imported goods and services for direct sale (automobiles, electronic devices and others). For all these imported goods and services, we must then pay more dollars, which we have to buy in the domestic exchange market, increasing the demand for dollars and pushing up the exchange rate and depreciating our peso against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The value of the exchange rate appears today as our great conjunctural concern. But it is of vital importance to control inflation, since a high percentage of it is explained by external factors.\par \par \uc2 In this regard, it can be stated that both the international economic situation mentioned above, as well as the inflationary and recessive expectations of the world economy, which surround it, are factors of uncertainty due to which economic and financial agents abandon the stock markets and take refuge in dollars, affecting the exchange rate of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with respect to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 This situation is expected to normalize in the short term, although in the meantime the stock market mood is not firm and some stock markets continue to fall and others recover slightly, basically because it is expected that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2 , FED, will raise the reference rate again to control its high inflation (9.1%), which would bring new impacts of depreciation of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. It will be necessary to remember that also the political uncertainty, at a national level, drives up the exchange rate in \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market is trying to control the strong expansion observed in the exchange rate in Chile, which in one month rose from 863.95 pesos per dollar (June 14) to $1,042.97 on July 14. It could be affirmed that the impact was materialized by the single announcement, which made the dollar fall to $972.40 the day after the announcement, Friday July 15, and achieved at the beginning of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sales in the exchange market, on Monday July 18, a reduction to $947.89, a value that includes for this day, an increase in the price of copper, which operated in the same direction as the official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , increasing the supply of dollars and making the dollar fall.\par \par \uc2 Then, apparently, the effects of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be very high and, perhaps, we will have rather high and undesired fluctuations of the exchange rate. These results show how difficult it will be to contain inflation in our country and the high costs that will surely be paid in terms of containing productive activity and increasing unemployment.\par \par \uc2 V\u237\'00\'EDctor Salas Opazo\par \par \uc2 Department of Economics\par \par \uc2 University of Santiago de Chile\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220721ei7l00dwe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
