\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Chile's Finance Minister seeks to calm investors after turbulent week\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Chile's Finance Minister \uc2 Mario Marcel\uc2  assured investors that the nation's giant copper industry would remain competitive despite plans to raise taxes, and that the new government would protect the country's reputation for fiscal discipline.\par \par \uc2 His comments come after a week in which the peso depreciated to record lows, in part due to falling copper prices and political concerns over a new constitution and tax law, before recovering after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  is approaching tax reform with an open mind," Marcel said in an interview with Bloomberg TV. The government needs to secure funding for its social program and at the same time investment in the copper industry. "We have a competitive mining environment and we don't want to damage it," he said.\par \par \uc2 Marcel is in New York this week as part of the Chile Day event, where government officials and private company executives promote investments in the country. This is the first Chile Day since the start of the pandemic and the issue of tax reform has come up in Marcel's meetings in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Chile's Congress will begin discussion of the tax bill on Wednesday. President \uc2 Gabriel Boric\uc2 's administration seeks to raise revenues equivalent to 4.1% of gross domestic product, mainly by increasing personal income taxes, creating a wealth tax and increasing mining royalties.\par \par \uc2 The tax reform has been criticized by local mining companies. \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  said earlier this month that, as initially proposed, it would weaken the sector's competitive position relative to global peers.\par \par \uc2 Peso advances\par \par \uc2 Marcel's visit comes just days after \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a US$25 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program to prop up the peso, prompting a 13% rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in just three trading sessions.\par \par \uc2 The move capped a turbulent week in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in which the peso extended its losses even after monetary policymakers raised the key interest rate by 75 basis points, more than many anticipated.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "very timely," Marcel said, as in the days leading up to the announcement volatility had risen to unjustifiable levels and interest rate differentials had widened.\par \par \uc2 Marcel also commented that the days of early pension withdrawals, in which Chileans took more than US$50 billion out of their savings during the pandemic, were a thing of the past.\par \par \uc2 "There was a big turnaround in the discussion on the latest pension withdrawal with a large majority against the measure," Marcel said. "The authorities and MPs realized that withdrawals create far more harm than good."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220720ei7k007sr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
