\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 LATIN AMERICAN MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  and stock markets close higher as global risk aversion eases\b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 (Updates closing values) By Froilan Romero SANTIAGO, Jul 18 (Reuters) - Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets rose on Monday, helped by increased investor appetite for risky assets that prompted a global retreat in the dollar as fears about the possibility of an aggressive increase in U.S. interest rates receded. * The dollar weakened Monday to a one-week low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after hitting two-decade highs last week, as traders trimmed bets on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressiveness in raising rates at its meeting this month. * A modest pickup in investor appetite for risk assets also weakened safe-haven demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . * Fed officials signaled Friday that they will likely raise rates 75 basis points at their July 26-27 meeting, even though a recent inflation measure may warrant larger-than-expected hikes later in the year.\par \par \uc2  * The dollar index, which compares the greenback against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , retreated about 0.4% and moved away from the two-decade highs it touched last week. * Gains were led by the Chilean peso, which climbed 3.12% to 940.50/940.80 units per dollar, its highest closing value in two weeks, boosted by the start of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which it will inject a total of 25 billion dollars into the market between July 18 and September 30. * Meanwhile, the leading index of the Santiago Stock Exchange, the IPSA, gave up important first hour gains and closed the day with a 0.19% drop to 5,082.07 units. * The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on Thursday that it agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market to prevent possible distortions in the face of the strong advance of the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, which took the peso to a historic low of 1,045.80/1,046.10 units per dollar. * Likewise, the Colombian peso strengthened by 1.22% to 4,305.65 units per dollar, although it rose by as much as 1.51% in the first minutes of the day. On the stock exchange, the MSCI COLCAP stock index advanced 0.49% to 1,281.71 points. * The Mexican peso was trading at 20.4608 per dollar at the close, with a gain of 0.30% against the Reuters reference price on Friday, in an environment marked by lower risk aversion and the weakening of the dollar, after disappointing inflation figures published last week in the United States. * The appreciation of the peso comes after the market began to reduce its speculation on the future of the U.S. monetary policy on Friday, after several \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials avoided making reference to the possibility of a 100 basis points increase in the interest rate, noted Banco Base. * The main stock index S&P;/BMV IPC, which comprises the 35 most liquid companies in the Mexican market, fell 0.32% to 46,923.20 units, with investors awaiting the publication of the companies' results for the second quarter and the next monetary policy decision of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . * The market is closely following the corporate reporting season, where the results of financial and technology companies stand out for this week, said Monex. * The Brazilian real depreciated close to the close by 0.55% to 5.4371 units per dollar, while the Bovespa index of Sao Paulo's B3 stock exchange climbed 0.38% to 96,916.13 points. * In Argentina, the peso fell 0.52% to 128.90/128.91 units per dollar in a depreciation regulated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Merval stock index rose 1.17% to 105,441.24 units, encouraged by a better external climate and local hedging in the face of growing inflation and exchange rate pressures. * Argentina's new Minister of Economy, Silvina Batakis, recently stated that she seeks to reduce the high fiscal deficit and fight inflation in an attempt to calm the financial markets. * The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, gained 0.36% to 3.890/3.891 units per dollar. Meanwhile, the Lima Stock Exchange benchmark added 1.55% to 467.07 points. Quotations at 2100 GMT Stock indexes Quotation Var pct Var pct daily in the year MSCI Emerging Markets 961.85 -0.37 -22.02 MSCI Latin America 1,951.25 2.02 -6.83 Bovespa Brazil 96,916.13 0.38 -7.5426 IPC Mexico 46,923.2 -0.32 -11.92 Argentina MerVal 105,441.24 1.169 26.28 COLCAP Colombia 1,281.71 0.59 -9.10 IPSA Chile 5.081.74 -0.2 17.96 Selectivo Peru 468.59 1.89 -8.54 Dollar vs. \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Quotation Var pct Var pct monthly in the year Brazilian real 5.4375 -3.34 2.46 Mexican peso 20.4570 -1.78 0.12 Chilean peso 942.9 -3.30 -10.07 Colombian peso 4.296.25 -3.58 -5.58 Peruvian sol 3.8913 -1.96 +2.04 Argentine peso 128.75 -2.86 -20.33 (Report by Froil\u225\'00\'E1n Romero. Additional reporting by Nelson Bocanegra in Bogota, Walter Bianchi in Buenos Aires and Vicente Valdivia. Edited by Marion Giraldo)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220718ei7i00abe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
