\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 LATIN AMERICAN MARKETS-Raise on lower risk aversion; Chilean peso leads gains\b0\par\par\uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 (Updates closing values) By Froilan Romero SANTIAGO, Jul 15 (Reuters) - Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets rose on Friday amid reduced investor aversion to risky assets, on a day when the Chilean peso posted a sharp gain after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sustained slide. * The dollar fell, as investors assessed the extent to which the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is likely to raise interest rates when it meets at the end of this month, and due to profit taking after a strong rise that took the greenback to a two-decade high in the previous trading session. * Gains in the region were led by the Chilean peso, which climbed 7.84% to 969.80/970.10 units per dollar and managed to overcome historical lows, to end the week with an advance of 0.72%. Meanwhile, the leading index of the Santiago Stock Exchange, the IPSA, fell 0.85% to 5,091.73 points.\par \par \uc2  * The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market for up to 25 billion dollars to prevent possible distortions in the face of the galloping advance of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. * In a statement issued on Thursday night, the agency said that in recent days the peso depreciation has occurred with unusually high intensity and volatility, which stresses the price formation of the exchange market. * The Colombian peso gained 2.96% to 4,359 units per dollar, while the stock market's benchmark index, the MSCI COLCAP, rose 0.1334% to 1,274.19 points. * The Mexican peso was trading at 20.5336 units per dollar at the close, with a gain of 1.23% against the Reuters reference price on Thursday, but accumulated a weekly decline of 0.44%, in its third consecutive week of losses. * The day was marked by a lower global risk aversion after the release of solid US economic figures and comments from \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  authorities that showed that they are still betting on a 75 basis point rate hike at their next meeting. * The main stock index S&P;/BMV IPC, which comprises the 35 most liquid companies in the Mexican market, gained 0.72% to 47,075.87 units, following the release of better-than-expected retail sales figures in the United States, dispelling some concerns about a recession, analysts said. * The Brazilian real appreciated 0.49% to 5.4059 units per dollar, while the Bovespa index of Sao Paulo's B3 stock exchange reversed initial losses and rose 0.6% to 96,696 points. * In Argentina, the peso fell 0.18% to 128.23/128.24 per dollar in a depreciation regulated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the Merval stock index gained 3.68% to 104,222.92 units, encouraged by the trend of the local Adrs traded in New York, in a cautious business context due to local economic doubts. * There is no mood in the market for the market to take off. There are many doubts and few certainties in the market, that is why the investor prefers to be liquid and covered", said a trader. * The Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sol, traded unchanged compared to the previous session, at 3.901/3.905 units per dollar. Meanwhile, the Lima Stock Exchange benchmark gained 0.48% to 459.92 points. Quotations at 2125 GMT Stock indexes Quotation Var pct Var pct daily in the year MSCI Emerging Markets 965.45 -0.52 -21.73 MSCI Latin America 1,912.59 -3.03 -8.68 Bovespa Brazil 96,551 0.45 -7.8909 IPC Mexico 47,075.87 0.72 -11.63 Argentina MerVal 104,222.92 3.685 24.82 COLCAP Colombia 1,274.19 0.13 -9.63 IPSA Chile 5.091.73 -0.85 18.19 Selectivo Peru 459.92 0.48 -10.23 Dollar vs. \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Quotation Var pct Var pct monthly in the year Brazilian real 5.4091 -2.82 3 Mexican peso 20.5200 -2.08 -0.18 Chilean peso 971.5 -5.96 -12.54 Colombian peso 4.349.6 -4.74 -6.72 Peruvian sol 3.8926 -1.92 +2.08 Argentine peso 128.22 -2.34 -19.91 (Report by Froil\u225\'00\'E1n Romero. Additional reporting by Luis Jaime Acosta in Bogota, Walter Bianchi in Buenos Aires and Vicente Valdivia. Edited by Javier Leira)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220715ei7f00bpc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
