\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 BofA\uc2  estimates that the rate will reach 10% in  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  this year and does not rule out  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 When the dollar touched $ 1,000 again, and after it was known that in June inflation advanced 12.5% in twelve months, all eyes are once again on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This Wednesday, \uc2 Bank of America (BofA) \uc2 will announce the adjustment that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will apply to the monetary policy rate (TPM), which is expected to be 50 basis points, leaving it at 9.5% .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. investment bank expects that Wednesday's adjustment will be followed by another of the same magnitude in September - higher than the 25 bps previously contemplated - which would raise the interest rate to 10% by the end of this cycle, above the 9.75% previously anticipated.\par \par \uc2 The financial entity expects "aggressive language" from the Board of the issuing institute in this week's meeting, as a result of the pressures and the Chilean peso's pass-through, and the higher inflation expectations in the latest surveys. "We expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to convey a higher rate peak and forward guidance, which will entail a delay in the expected rate cuts," reads a report released on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The analysis also recalls that the bank's corridor contemplated rate cuts since December. But \uc2 BofA\uc2  expects the downward adjustments to begin in March, "amid the looming recession," which would lead to the end of next year's MPR reaching 7.5%. In addition, the investment bank estimates that this year inflation will close at 11%, falling to 5.5% by the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 And the dollar? \uc2 BofA\uc2 's view is that the peso's weakness this year is due "mainly to weakening global fundamentals", as a result of the copper price falling 20% so far this year, the increase in oil prices and the increase in interest rates in the US.\par \par \uc2 "Chile has suffered one of the worst terms of trade shocks in the region - copper exports, oil imports - and we see the current account deficit rising to 7% of GDP this year," the bank warns.\par \par \uc2 The entity recalls that last year the pressure on the peso was "clearly explained by internal factors, including greater political uncertainty due to the political transition and the risks of the Constitution". Now, the agency considers that if the Magna Carta proposal is rejected -now its base scenario given recent polls- "this could provide some support to the peso, although we believe that the noise about changes to the Constitution will persist".\par \par \uc2 The analysis also states that while they do not rule out a dollar selling program by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this is not their base scenario either. "We see more likely a targeted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on days of excessive market fluctuations not driven by fundamentals and low liquidity," the report states, which specifies that some recent days saw "excessive fluctuation."\par \par \uc2 "We believe that the external weaknesses of the Chilean economy - very high current account deficit, private capital outflows - make it more difficult for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell a large amount of dollars," they detail, adding that "more aggressive language and slower cut guidance should also help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220711ei7b00az1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
