\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Lowering the dollar by secretariat?\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 At its June meeting, the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  raised its monetary policy rate by 75bp, above market expectations, generating global concern about the possible recession that could affect the world's main economy. In his testimony last Wednesday, Chairman Powell pointed out that both the interest rate hike and those to come could cause a recession, and that avoiding it depends on factors beyond the Fed's capabilities, with a nod to fiscal policy and banking supervision. He also reiterated that it is not possible to fail in this task and that inflation in the United States must return to 2% annually.\par \par \uc2 This scenario generated a substantial drop in the price of commodities, where copper has been substantially affected, falling by 16.7%, from US$ 4.55 per pound at the beginning of the month to US$ 3.79 per pound today, which implies an annualized loss of exports of US$ 9.6 billion. Additionally, it is usual for the prices of a large part of our export basket to be affected in correlation with what has been observed with the red metal. In turn, the financial angle of the rise in interest rates has raised the dollar index by close to 3%, which explains part of the devaluation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar and the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 However, both the real effect, the expected cycle for U.S. activity, and the financial effect precipitated a substantial devaluation of the Chilean peso, which rose from $857 per dollar in mid-June to $920 in the latest transactions, implying a drop of 7.3%. Given that nearly 70% of the CPI basket is made up of tradable goods, concern has arisen about the pass-through coefficient to domestic prices and the difficulty this implies for monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "Given that close to 70% of the CPI basket is composed of tradable goods, concern has arisen about the pass-through coefficient to domestic prices and the difficulty this implies for monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 In this context, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the liquidation of up to US$ 5 billion, with a daily maximum of US$ 200 million, for the next 60 days. This is not the first time that the Treasury carries out this action and in fact, in 2020 it sold US$ 15 billion, which rose to US$ 33.9 billion during last year. These sales respond to the cash planning of an institution that receives part of its income in dollars, while most of its expenses are in Chilean pesos.\par \par \uc2 Notwithstanding the above, it is necessary to specify this \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in greater detail, since the available balance in dollars in other Public Treasury assets at the end of April was US$ 2.7 billion, while the FEES and the FRP were US$ 14.3 billion. I imagine that the monies held in the latter two funds will not be used for dollar/peso cash programming.\par \par \uc2 Probably the worst expected effect of this decision is that the market will perceive it as an exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower the value of the dollar in \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This would be very unfortunate, since, given the overall characteristics of the phenomenon, it will not have a significant effect on the nominal exchange rate, as previous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the issuing institution have shown.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220628ei6s00855\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
