\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Wall Street's critical assessment of Chile\b0\par\par\uc2 1,884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Citi: "Political uncertainty casts doubt on whether fiscal policy will remain as prudent.Although Chile maintains solid macroeconomic fundamentals, Citi's economist following Chile, Fernando Jorge D\u237\'00\'EDaz, says political uncertainty sows a knob of doubt about the country's attractiveness as an investment destination, highlighting, for example, ongoing debates about the growth model and the pension system.\par \par \uc2 What's your general view of Chile's macroeconomic moment?\par \par \uc2 The risk of overheating has increased substantially this year. Although the economy is recovering from the downturn in 2020, fiscal policy has become even more expansionary.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, although commodity prices are very high and there are very favorable terms of trade for Chile, the peso is very depreciated. That's a combination that, in general terms, is quite inflationary. Moreover, if we look at the components of the inflationary rise, we see that the price of tradables, which are not very affected by the exchange rate, is rising. The inflation of the most stable prices, that is, excluding energy and food, is also rising.\par \par \uc2 There is a macroeconomic policy, especially fiscal policy, which is being procyclical and I don't know if it is worrying or not, but it will imply that in the future, when the economy has negative shocks again, the space to cushion them through expansionary monetary and fiscal policies will be smaller. That is the risk associated with the policies that are generating this overheating of the economy.\par \par \uc2 In this context, what would be the implications of a fourth withdrawal of the PFAs?\par \par \uc2 Regarding the impact on the cycle, if it is going to overheat the economy more or not, probably, each withdrawal has a lower impact on the product. Probably, more people won't spend that money because they already have liquidity than they didn't spend before. So the impact on the level of activity and the level of employment is probably marginal.\par \par \uc2 What we see with the different withdrawals is that each time the impact on the capital market is greater. At the beginning, the PFAs sold more liquid assets, mainly external, but with the pace of withdrawals they have to sell a little more domestic assets, which means that those withdrawals have a greater impact on the prices of financial assets.\par \par \uc2 The ability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to smooth that adjustment in portfolios is diminishing. This is also consistent with the fact that withdrawals are more disruptive to the domestic capital market.\par \par \uc2 Does the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have the capacity to contain rising inflation?\par \par \uc2 Yes. They have the necessary tools. Monetary policy acts with a lag, so it can no longer operate for the reasons that made inflation so high in September. So, in the short term inflation is going to be high. Fiscal policy was very different from what the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  calibrated in its base scenario and that is why inflation is rising today. In the medium term, with the CB acting decisively, it will manage to control inflation.\par \par \uc2 Has Chile lost its attractiveness as an investment destination?\par \par \uc2 Chile still has solid macroeconomic fundamentals. For example, if we were to measure the quality of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a stock, it's high, while debt - although higher - is still lower compared to other developing countries. What is true is that political uncertainty casts doubt on whether fiscal policy will remain as prudent as in the past, which adds to the question of what Chile's growth model will be.\par \par \uc2 Clearly there are unmet social demands, that's why the country is in this constitutional process - what's not clear is how those demands are going to be met. We've seen in other countries in the region that, in that, they compromise the growth model, which in the medium term is usually problematic. At the end of the day, if we look at which countries have improved their quality of life the most in recent decades, they are the ones that have grown the most, Chile being an example of this.\par \par \uc2 While all of them are valid, there are discussions today that were not there before. For example, there is the one related to the size of the State and the services it should provide to the population, which should include how this greater public spending is financed. There is also the discussion on what to do with the pension funds, raising the question of whether there is going to be a large public pension fund, whose criteria for the administration of its portfolio we do not know.\par \par \uc2 The challenges Chile is facing are evident and that is why asset prices are discounting greater uncertainty going forward.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 : "Today the market is demanding a higher risk premium than the country has been accustomed to".The Chilean economy is growing 5% above its potential level and an approval of a new withdrawal of the AFP fund "would exacerbate the imbalances," says Diego Pereira, economist for Chile at JPMorgan, based on calculations presented to PULSO. It is in this context that he also states that the market is valuing investment in Chile with greater risk.\par \par \uc2 What is your analysis of Chile's current macroeconomic situation?\par \par \uc2 Activity was 5.7% above the pre-pandemic level (6.8% if we exclude mining). Some 70% of the expansion is explained by the trade sector, 25% by services and only 12% by industry. This anatomy of expansion is typical of consumption booms and overstimulated economies, which is consistent with household income growing by about 27% of GDP since Covid came out, as of September this year, with pension withdrawals accounting for 78% of such accumulation of available liquidity. In our estimates, this has led the level of activity to be 5% above its potential level, which is consistent with the inflationary acceleration we are experiencing.\par \par \uc2 What would new withdrawals from the PFAs imply?\par \par \uc2 I defined the fourth withdrawal as flirting with a mini-financial crisis, self-created by impulses associated with politics and not the economy. And unfortunately we are seeing some of the effects associated with the increased likelihood that the Senate will end up approving such a measure. From a macroeconomic standpoint, a fourth withdrawal would exacerbate the imbalances discussed above, pushing inflation up, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation. Consumption would gain again, but the marginal impact would be smaller than the effect of the previous withdrawals when we think of the total impact on the level of activity. This is explained by higher inflation and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reaction by raising rates to avoid an even more damaging de-anchoring of expectations.\par \par \uc2 Does the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have the capacity to contain rising inflation?\par \par \uc2 In my baseline scenario, I discount a restrictive stance by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , taking rates to levels it considers neutral. But I see the path as very dependent on whether or not the fourth withdrawal is approved.\par \par \uc2 If approved, I see the policy rate at 3.75% and 6.5% by the end of 2021 and 2022, respectively. If not approved, at 3.25% and 5%.\par \par \uc2 Has Chile lost its attractiveness as an investment destination?\par \par \uc2 For financial investment, attractiveness is defined by the expected return, which is associated with risk. Today the market demands a higher risk premium than the country has been accustomed to, especially for assets denominated in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And we cannot forget that the premium associated with financial investment also impacts real investment, the key to growth. There can be scenarios with sustained growth and sub-optimal redistribution, as is argued to have occurred in Chile in recent years. But we cannot have sustained redistribution processes without growth.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 : "People are looking at Chile with some concern".The Chilean economy is seriously overheated, in the eyes of Goldman Sachs' chief economist for Latin America, Alberto Ramos. Part of that is explained, in his view, by an "overstretched" fiscal stimulus, precisely what calls into question Chile's short-term macroeconomic management and has begun to change the perception of the country's distinctly prudent political economy.\par \par \uc2 What is your general view of Chile's current macroeconomic situation?\par \par \uc2 It's a reality that the Chilean economy is overheated, the output ratio is already positive and it's going to stay positive throughout 2023, so it will continue to drive up inflation, although not all sectors are fully recovered. Certainly, there is heterogeneity, for example, in the labour market. However, the truth is that the economy is already operating above the natural limit of its capacity.\par \par \uc2 It is the fastest growing economy, it is the one with the highest level of vaccination and it has a huge fiscal stimulus. It would be necessary, then, to moderate the stimulus to consumption, so that the economy begins a path of more moderate, but sustainable and balanced growth.\par \par \uc2 In this context, what would be the implications of approving a fourth withdrawal from the PFAs?\par \par \uc2 The stimulus to consumption, through the fourth withdrawal of pension savings, would take place at a time when it is not necessary. What is required is to lower the pressure on inflation a little and also, in the medium and long term, it is important to have some savings so that people can withdraw them when they lose the capacity to generate income.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me that it would be prudent not to make the pension withdrawal now, given that at this cyclical moment in the economy less stimulus to consumption is needed.\par \par \uc2 How do you assess the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reaction?\par \par \uc2 The decision seems to me to be quite correct. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's objective is to lower inflationary pressure, because the economy is clearly overheating. If you don't control inflation you're going to have a much bigger impact on future growth, so raising the interest rate is a sign of a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that's quite determined and committed to reducing it.\par \par \uc2 You have to keep in mind that lowering inflation is social policy, because inflation is a regressive tax that makes it more difficult for low-income families. Therefore, it is very important, from a dynamic point of view, to lower inflation now so that we can return to growth at an acceptable rate and with a balanced economy in terms of monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Has Chile lost its attractiveness as an investment destination?\par \par \uc2 The thesis that Chile was a country that was positively differentiated from the rest of Latin America seems to be changing. That's no longer so true because short-term macroeconomic management itself, with fiscal stimulus a bit overdone, with high inflation and a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  having to validate an interest-rate shock to bring it down, shows that the "political economy" of fiscal management is changing quite a lot. It seems that we are going to go down the path of an economy with a greater presence of the State, more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There's a risk that it's veering away from a slightly more conventional and orthodox macro model, towards a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  and potentially more populist one.\par \par \uc2 People are looking at \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  with some concern. They don't know what's going to happen, but there's a question mark there that there wasn't in the past.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020211016ehag004zw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
