\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms EFE LT Foreign  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  and LT Local  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  IDR at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\b0\par\par\uc2 2,948 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2021\par 20:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 5 Aug 2021 14:36 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Affirms EFE LT Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and LT Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR at 'A-'; Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -S\u227\'00\'E3o Paulo-05 August 2021:\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  has affirmed \uc2 Empresa de los Ferrocarriles del Estado\uc2  (EFE) of Chile Long-Term Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (FC) Issuer Default Rating (IDR) and Long-Term Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  IDR at 'A-' with a Stable Outlook. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  also affirmed EFE's USD500 million 3.068% senior unsecured notes due in 2050 at 'A-', the same level as EFE's FC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Key Rating Drivers\par \par \uc2 Status, Ownership and Control Assessed as Very Strong\par \par \uc2 EFE is fully controlled by the state of Chile. A board of directors is appointed by the Chilean Economic Development Agency, which depends on the Ministry of Economy. \uc2 Chile's Ministry of Transportation\uc2  represents the formal interaction between EFE and the central government; defines the operating framework of EFE and prepares triennial investment plans jointly with EFE. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Treasury Ministry\b0 \highlight\uc2  approves investment plans, annual budgets and borrowing. EFE is subject to official audits by the Comptroller General of the Republic, the superior oversight body, and to the rules of the Comision Mercado Financiero (\uc2 CMF\uc2 ), the securities and insurance regulator in Chile.\par \par \uc2 Support Track Record Assessed as Very Strong\par \par \uc2 EFE has received consistent support from the government. EFE receives transfers from the nation for operating expenses (mainly maintenance cost) and capex. For both domestic and foreign debt, interest and capital payments are paid directly from the state of Chile to EFE's creditors. Every investment project has been done with federal transfers or through new debt. Local bonds are directly guaranteed by Chile. This support is expected to continue in the following years.\par \par \uc2 Sociopolitical Implications of Default Assessed as Strong\par \par \uc2 EFE's railroad network has the most extensive coverage and connectivity in Chile, which positions EFE as a critical link for the country's global supply chain. Given the social and economic discontent that could take place in Chile during an interruption of service, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  expects the state would try to continue providing service regardless of the contingency, event or debt default situation. The interruption of EFE will affect other entities due to its connectivity with ports and other means of transport, such as \uc2 Metro de Santiago\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Financial Implications of Default Assessed as Very Strong\par \par \uc2 Chile is in charge of paying EFE's debt service. The state covers the debt service without EFE's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A default of EFE would be comparable to \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own default and would lead to temporary closure of any market funding for both the state and EFE.\par \par \uc2 Standalone Credit Profile (SCP)\par \par \uc2 EFE's SCP assessed at 'b'\par \par \uc2 EFE's SCP is assessed at 'b', which reflects a Weak assessment for revenue defensibility, a Midrange assessment for operating risk, and a Weaker financial profile because leverage (\uc2 Fitch\uc2 's net adjusted debt to EBITDA) is over 100.0x in \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's rating case scenario. EFE's SCP also reflects peer comparisons.\par \par \uc2 The Weak revenue defensibility assessment reflects the highly subsidized tariff regime that does not allow for full cost recovery with revenues from core business activities alone, despite adequate passenger demand in recent years. As a result, EFE requires government transfers to close the gap between its own revenues and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs.\par \par \uc2 The Midrange operating risk assessment reflects the nature of the entity, characterized by high operating expenditure. EFE's operating expenditure registered a 2016-2020 CAGR of 5.7%, above the operating revenue CAGR of 3.7%. The result was mostly driven by the drop of revenues following the global pandemic and its impact on mobility and passenger flow. However, EFE was able to adjust operating expenditures by 9% in 2020, evidence of austerity amid a challenging environment. EFE presents an adequate supply of resources and labor with limited volatility. Capital planning management is assessed as neutral to EFE's operating risk.\par \par \uc2 EFE's net adjusted debt has increased in recent years and is expected to continue increasing given financing needs to implement the Chile sobre Rieles program. According to \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's rating case, it will exceed 100.0x EBITDA throughout the rating case scenario for 2021-2025. This is due to weak \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance and increasing debt, leading to a Weaker financial profile.\par \par \uc2 Debt Ratings\par \par \uc2 The ratings of EFE's USD500 million 3.068% senior unsecured notes due in 2050 is affirmed at 'A-', the same level as EFE's FC IDR.\par \par \uc2 Derivation Summary\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2  views EFE as a government-related entity (GRE) of the Chilean state and equalizes its ratings with those of the sovereign. This reflects a score of 50 points under our GRE Rating Criteria. The 'b' SCP does not affect EFE's IDR.\par \par \uc2 Under its GRE Criteria, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  classifies EFE as an entity linked to the Chilean government and applies an equalized approach based on its assessment of the strength of linkage with and incentive to support by the Chilean government. EFE's GRE support score is assessed at 50, reflecting a combination of a Very Strong Status, Ownership, and Control; Very Strong Support Track Record and Expectations; Strong Sociopolitical Implications of Default; and Very Strong Financial Implications of Default.\par \par \uc2 The 'b' SCP also reflects the peer comparison. The SCP assessment combined with a GRE Score of 50 leads to the 'A-' IDRs.\par \par \uc2 Key Assumptions\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's Key Assumptions Within Our Rating Case for the Issuer\par \par \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's rating case is a through-the-cycle scenario that incorporates a combination of revenue, cost and financial risk stresses. It is based on \uc2 Fitch\uc2 's sovereign group macro projections and incorporates the following assumptions:\par \par \uc2 --Revenue from core business to gradually recover to pre-pandemic levels until 2024;\par \par \uc2 --Operating costs will grow in line with historical performance, considering projected inflation;\par \par \uc2 --New debt in the amount of USD3.2 billion for 2021-2025, following EFE's expected disbursement schedule and financing need for Chile sobre Rieles.\par \par \uc2 RATING SENSITIVITIES\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to negative rating action/downgrade:\par \par \uc2 --EFE's Long-Term IDRs could be downgraded if the sovereign rating is downgraded, or on a weaker assessment of strength of linkage or incentives to support, leading to an overall score below 45 points under our GRE Criteria, which \uc2 Fitch\uc2  deems to have a low probability.\par \par \uc2 --Negative rating action on EFE's IDRs would result in similar action on the ratings of EFE's senior unsecured USD500 million notes due in 2050.\par \par \uc2 Factors that could, individually or collectively, lead to positive rating action/upgrade:\par \par \uc2 --Positive rating action on Chile's IDRs would result in similar action on the ratings of EFE and its senior unsecured USD500 million notes due 2050.\par \par \uc2 Best/Worst Case Rating Scenario\par \par \uc2 International scale credit ratings of Sovereigns, Public Finance and Infrastructure issuers have a best-case rating upgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a positive direction) of three notches over a three-year rating horizon; and a worst-case rating downgrade scenario (defined as the 99th percentile of rating transitions, measured in a negative direction) of three notches over three years. The complete span of best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings for all rating categories ranges from 'AAA' to 'D'. Best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings are based on historical performance. For more information about the methodology used to determine sector-specific best- and worst-case scenario credit ratings, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/re/10111579 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Liquidity and Debt Structure\par \par \uc2 Liquidity improved in 2020, driven by the recent issuance of USD500 million of international bonds in 2020. The pandemic delayed capex and new debt translated into higher cash for EFE. Considering investments are expected to pick up once mobility restrictions are phased out, we see the current improvement as temporary.\par \par \uc2 EFE's YE 2020 debt totaled CLP1,886 million, 78.2% of which is in local bonds, 18.4% international bonds and 3.4% in bank loans. The bonds schedule is bullet amortization and all local bonds have the state guarantee; international bonds and bank loans do not have a guarantee. However, interest and capital payments are paid directly from the state of Chile to EFE's creditors. While the substantial use of bullets is a negative factor for its debt structure, EFE's SCP is supported by the extended debt maturity profile and strong access to onshore and offshore capital markets.\par \par \uc2 Issuer Profile\par \par \uc2 EFE Group is the major infrastructure and transportation service provider in Chile. It is fully owned by the state of Chile, supporting the company's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , investment plan and debt service. EFE focuses on three segments: passenger, cargo transportation and real estate management.\par \par \uc2 ESG Considerations\par \par \uc2 Unless otherwise disclosed in this section, the highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3' - ESG issues are credit neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed by the entity.\par \par \uc2 REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\par \par \uc2 Public Ratings with Credit Linkage to other ratings\par \par \uc2 The ratings of EFE are supported by the ratings of Chile\par \par \uc2 Empresa de los Ferrocarriles del Estado\uc2  ; Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; A-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ----; Local \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Long Term Issuer Default Rating; Affirmed; A-; Rating Outlook Stable\par \par \uc2 ----senior unsecured; Long Term Rating; Affirmed; A-\par \par \uc2 Contacts:\par \par \uc2 Primary Rating Analyst\par \par \uc2 Fabio Bittar,\par \par \uc2 Director\par \par \uc2 +55 11 4504 2206\par \par \uc2 fabio.bittar@fitchratings.com\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Brasil Ltda.\par \par \uc2 Alameda Santos, n 700 -- 7 andar Edif\u237\'00\'EDcio Trianon Corporate - Cerqueira C\u23