\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Stocks Edge Higher; Peso Gains vs Dollar -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2020\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1657 ET - The IPC stock index inches 0.1% lower to 38707 Cemex falls 2% after the company said it expects to include a $1.5B impairment charge for 3Q related to goodwill at its US business and assets that have been idle for a long period or are expected to remain closed for some time. The charge won't affect liquidity or Ebitda, but will have an effect on assets and net profit, \uc2 Cemex\uc2  says. The peso strengthens to 20.86 against the US dollar from 20.96 yesterday, trading at its strongest level since the first week of March. Statistics agency Inegi reported a 2.5% rise in August retail sales, a fourth straight gain after April's slump but still 10% below year-ago levels. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1652 ET - The US dollar weakens 0.4% against the euro and 0.2% against the yen. The WSJ Dollar Index falls 0.2%. The dollar has lost momentum recently on hopes for another US fiscal stimulus package and news that Brexit negotiations will continue until mid-November, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. The firm also says the lack of an increase in risk aversion world-wide has stopped the euro and sterling from weakening against the safe-haven dollar even with rising Covid-19 cases. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1416 ET - Peru's President \uc2 Martin Vizcarra\uc2  has only a few months left in his term, but they will likely be full of volatility. After surviving an impeachment vote last month, opposition lawmakers are now planning to convene in November to consider another motion to oust him. That comes after newspaper El Comercio reported that Vizcarra was implicated by a witness in an alleged corruption scheme during his time as governor of the southern Moquegua state. He denies wrongdoing. Prosecutors say they will investigate, and political analysts say it is increasingly likely that Vizcarra will face legal troubles when he steps down in July. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1403 ET - Bolivia's incoming president, \uc2 Luis Arce\uc2  , will likely boost public spending, leading to bigger fiscal deficits as he aims to get Bolivia out of a deep economic contraction, says Oxford Economics. Arce, the former finance minister of ex-president \uc2 Evo Morales\uc2  , was elected on Sunday. Oxford says his economic policy probably won't change much from his time in the Morales administration, when poverty declined. Oxford notes his economic management was "marked by large public spending that failed to generate hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows and that didn't bolster the development of the private sector. This makes us doubtful about expecting something radically different from him." (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1346 ET - Chile will hold a referendum on Sunday to write a new constitution, which could "entrench greater state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy," \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. That could help \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy recover faster than others in Latin America; however, the constitution-writing process is also expected to fuel uncertainty, the firm says. "Political uncertainty is now the main certainty in Chile," it says. "Sunday's landmark referendum on constitutional change could be the start of a long and complex process to alter its political landscape." If the referendum is approved, Chileans will elect in April the assembly that will write the charter. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1128 ET - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep interest rates on hold in the short term as the ruble's weakness feeds through to higher inflation, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. Further interest-rate cuts are "off the table" for as long as inflation continues to rise toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4% target and the ruble remains "on the back foot," Capital Economics economist Liam Peach says. However, inflation will peak in the coming months and begin to fall in the first half of 2021, prompting the bank to cut rates by 25 basis points in February, he says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its policy rate at 4.25% Friday. USD/RUB last trades down 0.5% at 76.2478. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1047 ET - U.S. and eurozone third quarter economic growth figures due next week are viewed as backward looking so will probably have a limited impact on the euro-dollar exchange rate, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. Other data scheduled in coming days including durable goods orders, consumer confidence, personal income and personal consumption expenditure in the U.S. and the ifo's German business climate index will have a "greater impact" on EUR/USD, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  's Roberto Mialich says. The German business climate index is likely to show a "renewed decline" in business confidence, which should "curb any further EUR/USD rally much beyond 1.18 for now," he says. EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 1.1830. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 ET - The world economy has recovered about 60% of the output lost in the first half of the year, but recoveries in most parts of the world have already slowed and prospects now vary according to countries' success in controlling the virus, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. "The outlook for Europe has darkened as second waves have prompted new restrictions and fiscal support is being scaled back," says the forecasting firm. The U.S. looks more resilient, with risks in both directions relating to the virus and to the possibility of post-election fiscal stimulus. Inflation will stay very low everywhere, implying that the era of unprecedented monetary policy support is set to continue for at least the next couple of years, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1041 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's October 29 policy meeting is unlikely to materially impact the euro as further stimulus measures aren't expected until the end of the year, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. "[\uc2 ECB\uc2  ] President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  is expected to delay the announcement of further monetary policy expansion--in the form of more asset purchases under the pandemic emergency purchase program facility--to the final Monetary Policy Committee meeting of 2020 on December 10," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  's Roberto Mialich says. EUR/USD rises 0.2% to 1.1839.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1006 ET - The absence of an increase in global risk aversion has stopped the euro and sterling from falling considerably against the dollar despite rising coronavirus cases and Brexit risks, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. Given the second coronavirus wave and the threat of a no-deal Brexit, "one might have imagined" EUR/USD and GBP/USD would fall towards 1.16 and below 1.28, respectively, "if not further," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  's Roberto Mialich says. The "resilience" the two exchange rates reflects the dollar's role as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says. The dollar has "lost momentum" recently on hopes for another U.S. fiscal stimulus package and news that Brexit negotiations will continue until mid-November. EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 1.1833 and GBP/USD falls 0.2% to 1.3059. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0933 ET - Spain has replaced Mexico as the most vulnerable economy in the world to Covid-19, according to Oxford Economics. After Spain and Mexico, the Philippines, the U.S., and Brazil rank as the most vulnerable economies. On the other hand, economies like Switzerland, Australia, New Zealand and Mongolia remain the least vulnerable. "The global economy has become more vulnerable to Covid-19 with a 30% of growth in new cases since August," says Evghenia Sleptsova, senior economist at the forecasting firm. Big parts of the Northern Hemisphere are being afflicted by the second wave of the pandemic and this has far outweighed some improvements in epidemiology in some emerging markets, such as Brazil and India, Sleptsova says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0902 ET - A new U.S. administration under Democrat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  may hurt the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  's chances of agreeing a trade deal with the U.S. any time soon, as President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has backed the U.K. leaving the EU and emboldened the Brexit movement, Frederique Carrier at \uc2 RBC Wealth Management\uc2  says. Although Biden's position is less well known, the Obama administration opposed preferential trade terms for a U.K. "out of Europe," she says. Moreover, given the size of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  compared with the size of the U.K., Biden is unlikely to prioritize a U.K. trade deal. With U.S.-U.K. free trade deal talks on hold until spring because of the pandemic, Biden could pause all negotiations while re-examining U.S. trade policy, Carrier says. (anthony.orunagoriainoff@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 5:12 p.m. ET to show that the Mexican peso closed at 20.96 yesterday. An earlier version incorrectly said the peso closed at 21.96 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 23, 2020 16:57 ET (20:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020201023egan003ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
