\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2020\par 22:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1652 ET - The US dollar weakens 0.4% against the euro and 0.2% against the yen. The WSJ Dollar Index falls 0.2%. The dollar has lost momentum recently on hopes for another US fiscal stimulus package and news that Brexit negotiations will continue until mid-November, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. The firm also says the lack of an increase in risk aversion world-wide has stopped the euro and sterling from weakening against the safe-haven dollar even with rising Covid-19 cases. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1547 ET - Federal enforcers say \uc2 AT&T\uc2  's sale of Puerto Rican network infrastructure demands the divestiture of some fiber-optic cables and customer accounts to a third party, a sign the two telecom giants are near to closing their deal. The Department of Justice says it approved the spinoff to local provider WorldNet Telecommunications to protect competition in the US commonwealth. \uc2 AT&T\uc2  and \uc2 Liberty\uc2  last year agreed to the nearly $2B deal to help the former pay down debt. (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald)\par \par \uc2 1416 ET - Peru's President \uc2 Martin Vizcarra\uc2  has only a few months left in his term, but they will likely be full of volatility. After surviving an impeachment vote last month, opposition lawmakers are now planning to convene in November to consider another motion to oust him. That comes after newspaper El Comercio reported that Vizcarra was implicated by a witness in an alleged corruption scheme during his time as governor of the southern Moquegua state. He denies wrongdoing. Prosecutors say they will investigate, and political analysts say it is increasingly likely that Vizcarra will face legal troubles when he steps down in July. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1403 ET - Bolivia's incoming president, \uc2 Luis Arce\uc2  , will likely boost public spending, leading to bigger fiscal deficits as he aims to get Bolivia out of a deep economic contraction, says Oxford Economics. Arce, the former finance minister of ex-president \uc2 Evo Morales\uc2  , was elected on Sunday. Oxford says his economic policy probably won't change much from his time in the Morales administration, when poverty declined. Oxford notes his economic management was "marked by large public spending that failed to generate hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows and that didn't bolster the development of the private sector. This makes us doubtful about expecting something radically different from him." (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1346 ET - Chile will hold a referendum on Sunday to write a new constitution, which could "entrench greater state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the economy," \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. That could help \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy recover faster than others in Latin America; however, the constitution-writing process is also expected to fuel uncertainty, the firm says. "Political uncertainty is now the main certainty in Chile," it says. "Sunday's landmark referendum on constitutional change could be the start of a long and complex process to alter its political landscape." If the referendum is approved, Chileans will elect in April the assembly that will write the charter. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1128 ET - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep interest rates on hold in the short term as the ruble's weakness feeds through to higher inflation, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. Further interest-rate cuts are "off the table" for as long as inflation continues to rise toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4% target and the ruble remains "on the back foot," Capital Economics economist Liam Peach says. However, inflation will peak in the coming months and begin to fall in the first half of 2021, prompting the bank to cut rates by 25 basis points in February, he says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its policy rate at 4.25% Friday. USD/RUB last trades down 0.5% at 76.2478. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1047 ET - U.S. and eurozone third quarter economic growth figures due next week are viewed as backward looking so will probably have a limited impact on the euro-dollar exchange rate, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. Other data scheduled in coming days including durable goods orders, consumer confidence, personal income and personal consumption expenditure in the U.S. and the ifo's German business climate index will have a "greater impact" on EUR/USD, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  's Roberto Mialich says. The German business climate index is likely to show a "renewed decline" in business confidence, which should "curb any further EUR/USD rally much beyond 1.18 for now," he says. EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 1.1830. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 ET - The world economy has recovered about 60% of the output lost in the first half of the year, but recoveries in most parts of the world have already slowed and prospects now vary according to countries' success in controlling the virus, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. "The outlook for Europe has darkened as second waves have prompted new restrictions and fiscal support is being scaled back," says the forecasting firm. The U.S. looks more resilient, with risks in both directions relating to the virus and to the possibility of post-election fiscal stimulus. Inflation will stay very low everywhere, implying that the era of unprecedented monetary policy support is set to continue for at least the next couple of years, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1041 ET - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's October 29 policy meeting is unlikely to materially impact the euro as further stimulus measures aren't expected until the end of the year, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. "[\uc2 ECB\uc2  ] President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  is expected to delay the announcement of further monetary policy expansion--in the form of more asset purchases under the pandemic emergency purchase program facility--to the final Monetary Policy Committee meeting of 2020 on December 10," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  's Roberto Mialich says. EUR/USD rises 0.2% to 1.1839.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1016 ET - \uc2 Statistics Canada\uc2  estimates that travel restrictions imposed by Canadian authorities to contain the spread of Covid-19 had a direct cost on the country's tourism industry of up to C$23B and the loss of up to 406,000 jobs. The indirect impact on economic sectors that supply the tourism sector could lead to a further loss in GDP of up to C$13.8B and another 143,000 jobs. The data agency said travel restrictions will reduce GDP by up to 1.7% versus 2019, and account for over 14% of the total virus-induced decline in output. Tourism accounts for 2% of Canadian GDP. Canada has, among other things, mostly closed its border to foreign tourists, including the US. Meanwhile, provinces in Atlantic Canada have imposed their own restrictions on travel from within other parts of the country.(paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1006 ET - The absence of an increase in global risk aversion has stopped the euro and sterling from falling considerably against the dollar despite rising coronavirus cases and Brexit risks, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. Given the second coronavirus wave and the threat of a no-deal Brexit, "one might have imagined" EUR/USD and GBP/USD would fall towards 1.16 and below 1.28, respectively, "if not further," \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  's Roberto Mialich says. The "resilience" the two exchange rates reflects the dollar's role as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says. The dollar has "lost momentum" recently on hopes for another U.S. fiscal stimulus package and news that Brexit negotiations will continue until mid-November. EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 1.1833 and GBP/USD falls 0.2% to 1.3059. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0953 ET - Brazil's inflation was higher than expected in the month through mid-October, but the backdrop for consumer prices remains comfortable enough that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be able to stay calm in coming months, according to Alberto Ramos, an economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Brazil's statistics agency says Friday consumer prices rose 0.94% in the month through mid-October, the biggest increase for the period since 1995, as food prices increased rapidly. Airfares and the price of electronics and home appliances rose more than expected, but core and services inflation is still tracking at low levels, Ramos says. Brazil has a large output gap and high unemployment, which should help keep inflation under control going forward, Ramos says. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 23, 2020 16:52 ET (20:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020201023egan003wk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
