\par\par \b \uc2 Chilean  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  hopeful of no recession, will prevent peso panic - deputy governor\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2020\par 17:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Violent protests in Chile did major damage to economy\par \par \uc2 * Deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief sees demonstrations continuing\par \par \uc2 * Bank still ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "not uncomfortable" with current peso level\par \par \uc2 * Hopeful no need to cut growth forecasts further\par \par \uc2 * Chart of peso's troubles {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2ujYthh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2ujYthh}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcefully if violent protests threaten its peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, though it hopes the relative calm in recent weeks will be enough to keep the country out of recession, its deputy governor Joaquin Vial said.\par \par \uc2 Vial told Reuters in an interview that Chile's most widespread protests since its return to democracy in 1990 had done serious damage to the economy.\par \par \uc2 He is hopeful though that it will not be worse than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  feared in December when its slashed its 2020 growth forecast to a range of 0.5% to 1.5% from 2.75% to 3.75% previously.\par \par \uc2 "It looks to me that the estimate (for the economy) we made last quarter is very much in line with what happened," Vial said.\par \par \uc2 Asked if there was still a risk of a "technical" recession, defined as back-to-back quarterly contractions in the economy, he added: "If you look at the chart we are probably going to avoid that because the worst figures were from October and November."\par \par \uc2 "December is going to be very bad also, but we have seen some normalisation and the first quarter will most likely be somewhat better...But we have to see how fast confidence is going to restore."\par \par \uc2 The protests initially erupted in October after metro ticket prices were raised, but quickly escalated to subway stations being set alight and riots on the streets that left 26 dead and upwards of $1.5 billion in losses for businesses.\par \par \uc2 Vial warned that March and April could get rough again, with more student protests expected and then a referendum on replacing Chile's dictorship-era constitution.\par \par \uc2 If tensions do flair again though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared after last year's troubles saw the peso plunge 15%.\par \par \uc2 Vial said it had looked like getting "out of control" at one point, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in, putting $10 billion of its roughly $40 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on the table to stem the rout.\par \par \uc2 It required a number of emergency meetings but it worked. It ended up using about $2 billion of its reserves and the peso made back some ground, but the bank is still on alert.\par \par \uc2 "We have signalled very clearly that we may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there is disruption again."\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Vial stressed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not want to change the "trend" of the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "Among other things we think this is part of the normal reaction to the new reality of the Chilean economy," he said referring to the sharp shock to growth. It could be argued that is should be lower, but "we don't feel uncomfortable with this level of the exchange rate," he added.\par \par \uc2 On interest rates, which did not change during the protests, there are opposing forces. The economy is under stress, but the sharp drop in the peso means inflation will soon be nearly 4%, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preferred level of 3%.\par \par \uc2 "We don't feel the depreciation has created the pressure to raise rates," Vial said.\par \par \uc2 However, with the risk of more peso weakness if demonstrations become violent again, he added: "from my personal point of view, (a rate cut) is less likely that the opposite option."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Marc Jones; Editing by Angus MacSwan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-1-22T17:26:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200122eg1m03bod\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
