\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Two months of social crisis, financial indicators show signs of recovery, but productive sectors remain lagging behind\b0\par\par\uc2 3,709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The main local stock index, IPSA, rose 1.7% in 2019 to October 18. The outbreak of the social crisis left that performance behind and dyed the local market uncertainly.Thus, ipSA fell 13.58% in the year and on November 13 reached a two-and-a-half-year low by closing by 4,412.25 points. That day was the turning point for the local stock market, as it has shown an uptick from there, although it is still 7.3% below the pre-crisis level. Yesterday it closed at 4,814.68 points.Since the social outburst, the most affected IPSA shares are \uc2 Salfacorp\uc2 , which has declined by 38.78%; Ripley, who is down 34.52%, and Entel, who has fallen 27.72%.\par \par \uc2  Despite these casualties, analysts note that local stocks have had a recovery in recent weeks, given greater local stability and a decrease in violence."The situation of violence and instability has tended to decline in the country; Chileans' lives return to the pre-social outburst routine, which normalizes economic activity in the country," says MBI equities analyst S\u233\'00\'E9bastien Fresard.This has been accompanied, according to experts, by a political environment that seeks to clear uncertainty, reaching agreements around the main demands of the public.However, Pablo Sol\u237\'00\'EDs, head of institutional equities at Tanner Investments, notes that "agreements on sensitive issues for citizens are still lacking. If strong consensus is reached this summer, we should expect to have more visibility into domestic economic behavior and how that performance will affect corporate performance by 2020."After peaking at $829, the dollar has fallen and the Chilean peso is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that most appreciates against the dollar in DecemberIt has been 61 days since the social outburst and the markets have suffered its effects. Uncertainty that has been generated regarding the economy and new regulations led investors to seek the dollar as a safe asset, causing it to climb to all-time highs of $829 in late November.However, in recent weeks it has recovered. In December it has fallen $53.4 and the Chilean peso is the most appreciated emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar with an advance of 7.17%. Despite this drop, the exchange rate is still far from the $712 levels it was in prior to the social outburst.Yesterday, the exchange rate dropped $5.8 and closed at $756.6 since November 8 when it ended at $747.5.According to analysts, several events have led to the fall of the dollar. These include the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the decrease in violence alongside the progress in legislative agreements in pursuit of the social agenda announced by the Government and, most recently, the increase in the price of copper under the agreement of the "phase one" between the United States and China.Felipe Bravo, strategy and studies manager of Sartor Finance Group, comments that "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with the legislative-political progress and the decrease in events of extreme violence are elements that underpin the strengthening of the weight."On the international level, Banmerchant Capital General Manager Luis Mendez notes that it influenced "the rise in the price of copper after the signing of phase 1 of the China-U.S. trade agreement."Moving forward, analysts predict that the exchange rate will continue to fall below the current level to stabilize at around $750.S\u233\'00\'E9bastien Fresard, MBI's equity analyst, says "should stabilize at these levels, between $750 and $760. We also believe that at these prices the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will begin to withdraw some of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (as it has done every Friday). This should stopto fall and keep it stable around these levels."While expectations are positive and some recovery of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is shown, Banmerchant's Mendez says that uncertainty levels will remain high in the face of the new Constitution.Country risk downgraded by political agreementsAlmost 60 points came the country risk measured by the CDS to 5 years in the context of social and political crisis that the country is going through. Thus, since 18 October, this indicator has risen 22%, but so far in 2019 it has a decrease of 35.86%. Yesterday it dropped 6.13% and closed by 40.18 points, away from the 57.43 peak points that marked November 29 along with the dollar rising to historic levels and ended with the exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .Pablo Sol\u237\'00\'EDs, head of institutional equities at Tanner Investments, points out that country risk is an indicator that has tended to stabilize. "We consider that it is unlikely to see an additional deterioration to solvency in the country. With an autonomous \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a well-functioning financial market, the likelihood of seeing a structural change in the rules of the game is low," says Sol\u237\'00\'EDs.The fall in the country's risk is due, according to Felipe Bravo, strategy and studies manager of Sartor Finance Group, to "actions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, political agreements and reduction of violence along with better prospects for emerging countries and exporters of raw materials".Rising and recovering financing costsA significant milestone marked on Tuesday the long-term local financing costs measured by one of the market benchmarks: 10-year \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds in UF (BCU 10). Their rates fell sharply 24 basis points, to 0.25%, below the 0.26% they were marking on October 18 last year, when the social outburst was exposed."Overall, the mood is calming down and this social explosion has been falling down: it looks more governance and that has stuck not only to the rates, but also to the stock market," says Milenko Mitrovic, fixed income manager at Octogone \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Add to that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income markets," he says.In the market they believe that the key lies in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has led the dollar to fall $72.4 from its all-time high ($829). As of this Tuesday (when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped $5.8 to $756.6), the monetary body had injected $1.95 billion in spot reserves and $2.85 billion in forward hedges in nearly two and a half weeks of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In addition, the entity had purchased $2,758 million in its own bonds from the market as of last Thursday. It is the so-called Creole QE, or Central's debt-buying program, which is intended to contain financing costs.The leak from Chilean stock fundsThe first economic signs of the social crisis had a rapid impact on the market: small investors quickly reduced assets - both bonds and shares - in their cards. This was evident in mutual funds, instruments whose flows capture changes in small investors' preferences. During the eight weeks following October 18, funds investing in local stocks saw many bailouts and very few contributions. This led to persistent net uninvestments. The amounts of these weekly sales, which totaled almost US$ 250 million, reached a peak in mid-November, after which they were regressing, until last week: the first since the beginning of the crisis that Chilean stock funds managed to capture more contributions that ransoms, which gave them their first positive balance. It's a timid $859 million, but it could be the basis for new advances.Exports20%, but is attenuated in DecemberThe performance of foreign trade has been impacted by the social crisis that is going through the country, both by the instability of the exchange rate and by the partial work that was recorded a few days in ports and transport Terrestrial. Between the week ended October 23 and December 7, exports fell 20% compared to the same period in 2018.In the first post-crisis week, local exports fell 39%, while imports fell 33%. However, these setbacks have tended to slow, especially in the case of shipments. According to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics, exports of goods between 1 and 7 this month fell 6.6% compared to the same week last year, while imports fell 29.4% over the same period.On the export side, one of the products that has helped mitigate the decline is fruit shipments, explains Banchile chief economist Carolina Grunwald. "Cherry season, for example," explains the economist.He adds that, in general, foreign trade had been slowing down before. "Decisions to ask for things are made from the before," he points out.It analyses that November came with foreign trade information with "the crisis" so that the commissions of that month were affected by the uncertainty it generates, in addition to the likely less need for products.Employment impact: Dismissals for the needs of the company reach 110 thousand since the beginning of the crisisOne of the most sensitive blows that economists have anticipated by the social crisis is the one that has received the job. Between 18 October and 13 December, dismissals for Article 161(1) of the Labour Code, which refers to the causal "needs of the undertaking", reach 110,630 jobs.Although there are some indicators that have tended to recover in recent weeks, it still does not show an improvement. In fact, in the first 13 days of December alone, dismissals for the company's needs amounted to 35,836, very close to the monthly average of 40,313 this year between the months of January and September before the social outburst.The Undersecretary of Labour, Fernando Arab, comments that "in the first fortnight of December we already reached the average layoffs for the needs of the company of a normal month. It is therefore unfair for workers to suffer the effects of the crisis by losing their jobs. That is why it is so important, on the one hand, to curb violence, as many of the jobs lost respond to employers who were vandalized and, on the other hand, it is key to move rapidly on the agenda of economic revival and employment protection."Since the social outburst on 18 October, November is the month that has had the most layoffs for this cause, ranking at 62,049 compared to 33,628 that he scored the same month in 2018.In its last Monetary Policy Report (IPoM) in early December, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimated that given the country's crisis, unemployment will record a sharp increase, and could reach 10% early next year. In the August-October mobile quarter, the last available data, the unemployment was 7%.Commerce begins to normalize its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and active demolition of burning rooms after relocating its workersEven as the economic sector most affected by the social crisis, two months after the events have started - which have involved stinging, damage and even fires in its buildings, its main players have already begun, as far as possible, to normalize its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the country.This is the case of the Sodimac home improvement chain, linked to the soa-wingFalabella, who said that of the 89 stores it operates in Chile, at this time of year "almost all of it are \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  (only a Homecenter, a Builder and a store of the Imperial subsidiary are closed, as they were affected by fires)", he said.Regarding the future of the burned enclosures, which were located on leased land, the company said that "we have not yet defined the investment plan", so its reconstruction is still being evaluated. However, he explained that "we have relocated all the workers in the stores that were burned."In the case of the Tottus supermarket chain, which is also part of Falabella, they indicated that 12 of its 69 premises were significantly affected. The firm even stated that "there are two supermarkets where the damage was significant and of great severity, which has involved making the decision to keep them closed."These are located in Llolleo, in the town of San Antonio and Pedro Fontova, in the commune of Huechuraba, Santiago.All of the above affected 2,000 workers of the company, of which 90% were relocated to other rooms.In the case of SMU, Unimarc's parent, Wholesale 10, Alvi and OK Market, stated that "95% of our premises are operating to date. In relation to burning premises, we are initiating demolition processes in those that require it. The company has not made any other decisions."Pharmacies Ahumada has also begun to normalize its functions. Of the 341 premises operating in Chile, 305 are already \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while 36 remain closed.Even with this process of reactivation of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Santiago Chamber of Commerce (CCS) analyzed that "the holiday season has been very weak, despite the fact that in recent weeks there is a moderate improvement in demand". Thus, he predicted "a drop of around 1% in sales this year, as a result of the last quarter crisis, and for now a flat 2020 year."On employment, according to a recent CCS survey, 18% of those over the age of 18 said that at least one person in their household has lost their jobs since the social crisis erupted.Construction continues with braking plans and notes that "it is far from saying that tranquility has returned"One of the most obvious reactions of the social outburst in the construction and real estate sector was the freezing of the start of investments whose works were about to begin. Added to this was the industry's recognition of a decline in sales, which has affected 70% of companies, according to a recent survey by the Chilean Chamber of Construction that adds that more than a third of firms admit that they have cancelled contracts and that their dot has been reduced.Productivity in the ongoing work has also declined, especially in completion work and in the first weeks after 18 October. Meanwhile, coarse-work activities will be resented in a few months when the crews that are now in these activities see the labor supply reduced because of a shortage of new projects, they indicate in the sector.Regarding the vision of the item two months after the social outburst, the executive director of the Association of Real Estate Developers (ADI), Vicente Dom\u237\'00\'EDnguez, notes that "my appreciation is that after the worst moments of the social upheacon, the situation is not appreciates as critically as a few weeks ago; however, it is far from saying still that the tranquility has returned. As we know, the investment will return if calm returns and the state manages to take back control of public order, which has lost it. If this does not happen, the fall in investment will continue."Dominguez adds that "there are sensitive lows in real estate sales, which are approaching 50%. But, for example,on the other hand, it is clear that a significant housing deficit is still being maintained and needs to be built, which should lead to steps in the way of taking steps to make this possible."The constitutional debate and within that the social role of housing also concerns the sector. "The discussion around the role of social housing is handled more by ideological convictions than by technical evidence. Ordinarily, experiences in other countries are known in a twisted way to justify positions," says Dominguez.He admits that "there is, of course, concern for constitutional discussion, but the central themes of the debate are not yet seen; for example, whether it will be a more presidential or parliamentary government, whether the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be maintained, whether or not there will be a Constitutional Court, and so on."In tourism they have not managed to revive the demandA critical outlook. This is how the executive vice president of the Federation of Tourism Companies (Fedetur), Helen Kouyoumdjian, described the situation in tourism. The sector has been one of the main areas resentful of the effects of the social crisis, which two months after its start, fails to rebound.According to the latest figures from the Undersecretariat of Tourism, 292,215 foreign visitors arrived in November, 37% less than in the same month of 2018. It is the largest year-on-year low recorded since the coming of international tourists."We still don't see an uptick in bookings. Initially there was a nearly 50% cancellation of bookings and a significant drop in demand, that has not improved at all," Kouyoumdjian said.On what is expected for December, the executive of Fedetur pointed out that the fall will not be so pronounced, mainly because last year there was a lower tourist arrival explained by the decline of Argentinians. "But even few people are going to get there, it will be a figure well below the historical ranges of the month of December," he said."We wanted to have a hope, campaigning various lycampaigns, but from an economic point of view we are still in the critical state of a month ago," he added.Fedetur estimated that 2019 will close with a 23% drop in the influx of foreign tourists. This means that a total of 4,390,000 people from other countries will visit the country, compared to 5,722,928 who arrived in 2018.Kouyoumdjian projected that the sector could return to normal in about four to six months more. "It will all depend on how the security conditions are provided, if there are no longer disturbances where the tourist infrastructure is. That's where we should be able to reactivate," he said.Demand for energy recovers and grows 0.4%An improvement presented demand for energy as of November, according to data revealed yesterday by the National Electricity Coordinator. This, after the social outburst that has affected the country.According to the end of November, electricity consumption in both the industrial and residential sectors rebounded and showed a slight growth of 0.4% compared to the same month of the previous year, reaching 6,286 GWh in gross energy demand. The above shows a slight revival of post-explosion consumption.Recovery goes hand in hand with the lukewarm normalization of trade and services such as the subway, as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  continuity achieved by miners, whose consumption accounts for 35% of national demand.However, there is also an effect associated with the fact that the eleventh month of the year had one more business day than in the previous year, where there were two holidays, playing in favor of a better monthly performance.The importance of this most encouraging figure lies in the correlation between electricity demand and economic growth, after a month of October inthat the indicator fell 2.2% compared to the same period in 2018.However, from the coordinator they noted that there have been no problems with the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the system, and according to the figures they presented, the cumulative growth in energy demand in the January-November period is a lean 0.3%.Sales of new vehicles would decrease by 12% this year and the sector estimates that in 2020 they will fall backThe disruption of public order was the main factor that in the last two months affected the sales of vehicles in the country. In the first weeks after October 18, locals closed earlier due to demonstrations, while difficulties in transporting the city led to customers not being able to reach the concessionaires. That, coupled with the fear of moving through streets with violent protests.Added to this is the oscillating price of the dollar, which in some days in the last 60 days exceeded $800, motivating the item to predict an increase in new vehicles. But stocks such as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the decline in public unrest, and the progress of a China-U.S. trade agreement have brought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at lower prices.Last October, sales of new vehicles fell 24.5% annually, according to figures from Chile's National Automotive Association. In November they rushed further and dropped 27.6% compared to the same month in 2018, totaling 24,272 units.Between January and November of this year, 341,788 new vehicles have been marketed, 10.5% less than in the same period last year. In this context, 2019 would close with 368 thousand units sold, a decrease of almost 12% compared to the record of 417,038 units marketed in 2018. For the next financial year, companies in the sector estimate a further decline, as they would be between 250 thousand and 300 thousand zero kilometer.In the fruit sector they operate normallyThe president of the Federation of Fruit Producers, Jorge Valenzuela, explained that in the fruit sector they are operating normally. He noted that, initially, there were road closures and mobilizations that generated isolated problems in the logistics of fruit production and distribution, but failed to cause large impacts.However, he acknowledged that the picture was complicated during the week of port shutdown in late November, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the San Antonio terminal was frozen."The mood was warmer and as a consequence there was more uncertainty," he said.The trade union leader argued that, rather than the effects of the social outburst, one problem of concern in the field is water scarcity. "The most critical thing is the water and that has a productive problem because with less water the fruit sizes are getting smaller," he said.Industry: \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been stabilizingSince the Association of Metallurgical and Metal-mechanical Industries (Asimet), explain that the productive activities in the metallurgical and metalworking industry have tended to be normalized in these 60 days since the social outburst, but that the uncertainty factor continues to dominate among entrepreneurs in the sector. "Since 18 October, like all companies, the situation in the first few weeks was very strong because of the rants and violence that affected business and public transport. The biggest drawback was the difficulties of moving workers to their workplaces and homes," said Dante Arrigoni, president of Asimet.According to the leader, in recent weeks the situation has been shifting towards a more positive one, but "the uncertainty factor remains very strong in terms of investment and growth from next year,which will also impact employment," Arrigoni added.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020191218efci003l6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
