\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  from Chile, Colombia and Brazil touch historic lows against the dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Chile, Colombia and Brazil hit all-time lows against the dollar this week due to political instability, outstanding reforms, popular demands for social equity and growing war-on-account uncertainty non-trepy.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar has been strengthened by a nervousness in the face of the possible contagion of political instability in the region," announced on Thursday in an interview with Efe, Latin America's chief analyst at \uc2 OANDA\uc2 , \uc2 Alfonso Esparza\uc2 , who believes that "inequality has crawling in the slow progress of the economy" leading to these social manifestations.\par \par \uc2 According to the spokesman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and "commodities" firm, the devaluation also comes to "the attractiveness of the dollar" as a refuge from the uncertainty of the trade war and "a solid advance" of the US economy "when most developed and emerging economies remain stagnant."\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real, the Colombian peso and the Chilean peso hit new all-time lows against the dollar this week.\par \par \uc2 A scenario in which Latin America's central banks could cut rates even further to boost economic growth in the face of low inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 These are the keys to the movement of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region.\par \par \uc2 1.BAD DAYS FOR THE CHILEAN PESO\par \par \uc2 The weakening of Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaked in Chile, where the peso depreciated on Thursday by 1.1% and closed at 828.36 units per dollar, its lowest value in history.\par \par \uc2 This slip of the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coincides with the 42 days since the beginning of the violent social outburst in the country that still shows no signs of a nearby exit.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today announced an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market that will consist of the sale of dollars in the amount of up to 10 billion spot and exchange hedging instruments for another 10 billion.\par \par \uc2 The issuer also lowered its growth forecast for 2019 from 2.5 to 1.9%. The Government estimates that up to 300,000 jobs could be lost by the end of the year, which would raise the unemployment rate from 7% to 10%.\par \par \uc2 2. MEASURES THAT DO NOT CONVENCE\par \par \uc2 Colombia has been facing a wave of protests since November 21 against President Ivan Duque's economic and social policies.\par \par \uc2 According to analysts, the demonstrations are beginning to have an impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and in fact this week the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit all-time highs in that country.\par \par \uc2 The dollar valued a little over 1% against the Colombian peso on Wednesday and hit a new all-time high by achieving a trading cap of 3,524.\par \par \uc2 And in today's session the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit 3,520 per unit, but it eventually closed at 3,508.\par \par \uc2 A report released on Thursday by \uc2 OANDA\uc2  notes that "investor nervousness is rising the longer unemployment is," which does not seem to be diminishing in the face of the disconnect between the Colombian government and the leaders of the demonstrations that have already expressed to the what their conditions for negotiating are.\par \par \uc2 3.AFTERTHE STORM...After three consecutive record-breaking slamming, the dollar relinquished 1% in Brazil and was posted to 4,215 reais on Thursday thanks to a new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This fall comes after the Brazilian issuer auctioned $1 billion on the market to contain the greenback hike.\par \par \uc2 Alfonso Esparza\uc2  states that "the risk of contagion of protest movements" in Brazil "has forced the government of Jair Bolsonaro" to curb "the reforms planned to avoid triggering a confrontation with groups opposed to the measures".\par \par \uc2 Yesterday the US dollar hit a new all-time high in Brazil, after it appreciated 0.45% against the Brazilian real and ended the day sold at R$4,258 in the trade exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 4.A WEEK WITHOUT MAJOR UPS\par \par \uc2 In Argentina, despite the relentless devaluation of the last year, the dollar remained stable on Thursday for the second consecutive day and stood at 57.75 pesos for the purchase and 62.25 pesos for sale at the state-owned \uc2 Banco Naci\u243\'00\'F3n\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Argentine economist Gustavo Ber explains that one of the market concerns has to do with Argentina's debt restructuring, following the urgency of the "bulging" maturities of the coming months.\par \par \uc2 By 2020, the South American country faces debts to private creditors and multilateral agencies of about $39.3 billion.\par \par \uc2 5.A DOLLAR THAT IS STRENGTHENED\par \par \uc2 During today's day, the US dollar appreciated 0.05% against the Mexican peso and was exchanged at 19.56 per unit after "solid data" in the U.S. economy and "an increase in turbulence" in the possible U.S.-China trade treaty, according to \uc2 OANDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been depreciating all week with a strong dollar, on the one hand, and on the other the growth revision that remains stagnant. (...) The Mexican peso depreciated 1.83% in November and continues to press down for the same factors that have led to all-time highs in other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says.\par \par \uc2 By: Daniel Salazar Castellanos\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020191129efbt00100\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
