\par\par \b \uc2 Barron's Blog:Peru Better Exposure Than Chile: Peruvian Sole Lower Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2013\par 15:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on Barron's Online's Emerging Markets Daily at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Shuli Ren\par \par \uc2 As we have seen last Friday and in Asia today, fear of September QE tapering has driven EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. Peruvian sol should be no exception. In a research note published today, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  lowered their forecast to 2.85 sol per dollar by 2013 year end, and to 2.95 in 2014. Peruvian sol last traded at 2.8 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But Peruvian sol is seen as a relatively low-risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  BCRP is more willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier this month, BCRP bought its sol in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend a 2.8 sol-per-dollar ceiling.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts Alejandro Arreaza, Alejandro Grisanti, and Sebastian Brown's own words:\par \par \uc2 BCRP is more likely to manage the PEN's volatility than the BCCh [\b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] is of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the CLP [Chilean peso] market. As a result, we do not think the USD/PEN is likely to go above 2.90. On the other hand, the USD/CLP does not have a ceiling, and while we expect the USD/CLP to only rise by about 3% over the next six months, it could touch levels above 520 in the event of a global shock. In short, the USD/CLP does not have a ceiling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk is a big concern for US investors holding emerging market single-country ETF's. We have two countries: Peru and Chile - both exposed to China and mining and both exhibiting some signs of internal economic strength. The IMF forecast Peru's GDP to grow at 6.1% in 2014 and Chile's to grow at 4.6%. Meanwhile, the two countries' CPI inflation, current account deficits, and unemployment rates forecasts are remarkably comparable. See IMF forecast (table 2.4, p.60) here.\par \par \uc2 Exchange rate differential can be a main driver of the pricing then, if Peru and Chile have similar fundamentals. Year-to-date, \uc2 iShares MSCI All Peru Capped ETF\uc2  ( EPU) lost 22.9%. \uc2 iShares MSCI Chile Capped ETF\uc2  ( ECH) lost a comparable 22%.\par \par \uc2 Last week, EPU gained 3.1%. ECH lost 3.3%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from Barron's, see Barrons.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://barrons.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://barrons.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 19, 2013 09:19 ET (13:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130819e98j003fv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
