\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico, Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  halt sell-off\b0\par\par\uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2013\par 22:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2  * Concerns about end of U.S. stimulus weigh on some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * Mexico peso gains 0.3 percent, Brazil real ends little changed \par \par \uc2  RIO DE JANEIRO, May 20 (Reuters) - Latin America's most traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied or posted some gains on Monday as investors reassessed the magnitude of their recent losses.\par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso and the Brazilian real posted a string of losses over the past few days as fears that the U.S. Federal Reserve could reduce or even close its bond-buying program drove investors away of risk assets in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2  An eventual end to the Fed's stimulus measures would remove a key source of global liquidity, which fuels dollar flows into higher-yielding emerging economies.\par \par \uc2  "Over the past few sessions the real has weakened in sync with other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but today we're seeing a relief, a correction," said Jose Carlos Amado, a trader with Renascenca brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2  * Mexico's peso gained 0.3 percent after seven straight sessions of losses led it to a near three-week low.\par \par \uc2  * Brazil's real  was little changed at 2.0388 per dollar after four consecutive losing sessions took it to a near four-month low, increasing chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso continued its slide, however, and fell for a tenth consecutive session to a fresh year low of 483.00 per dollar. "If we look at fundamentals, they are all favorable to the peso, with copper prices and the euro higher," said a trader in Santiago, adding that outflows by foreign investors were weighing on the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  * Concerns of the Fed tampering with its stimulus program also hit Mexican bonds. Yields on the benchmark 10-year peso bond bid 6 basis points higher to a one-month high of 4.73 percent. The bond's yield has spiked up 31 basis points from a record low hit on May 9. \par \par \uc2  Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2000 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                2.0390    -0.06     0.02\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.3045     0.31     4.55\par                                           \par Chile peso               483.0000    -0.41    -0.89\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1842.9000    -0.03    -4.17\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.6420     0.04    -3.44\par                                           \par Argentina peso             5.2425    -0.10    -6.29\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             8.8500     1.13   -23.39\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Walter Brandimarte in Rio de Janeiro, Natalia\par Cacioli in Sao Paulo, Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City, Froilan\par Romero in Santiago; Editing by Dan Grebler)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\u